    "The Drowned Temple": {
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "name": "The Drowned Temple",
      "radius": 5,
      "region": "Muirscáth",
      "x": -10,
      "y": 25,
      "basicInfo": "A pre-Collapse ruin submerged beneath the harbor — an ancient temple of unknown origin whose stone corridors fill and drain with the tide, creating a dungeon that is only accessible during low water and only survivable with water-breathing magic. The Temple's carvings depict beings that match no known pantheon or demonology, and the artifacts recovered from its outer chambers are powerful, strange, and faintly wrong in ways that scholars cannot articulate. The Guild classifies it as A-rank and requires S-rank escort for any expedition past the tide line. The smart money says there are things down there that should stay down there.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The beings depicted in the carvings are not from Aerfála at all — they are from the space between worlds, the same void that isekai arrivals cross. The Temple was a waystation for inter-dimensional travelers, and it still functions. The seal is not keeping something out — it is keeping the doorway shut.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "The Hearth Springs": {
      "areas": {
        "garden-path": {
          "description": "A winding path through gardens that bloom with whatever the Demi-Plane's ambient magic produces — flowers from the creator's memories, plants that remind residents of home, and occasionally something entirely new that the dimension invented on its own. Benches and quiet corners make this a place for private conversations and contemplation. The garden grows more elaborate as recreation develops.",
          "paths": [
            "hot-spring"
          ]
        },
        "hot-spring": {
          "description": "A natural hot spring that emerged from the Demi-Plane's potential when recreation development began. The water is warm without a heat source, clear without filtration, and faintly luminescent in dim light. Stone benches line the edges. Steam rises in curling patterns that the Chamberlain swears are not intentional but look suspiciously like the creator's name. Very Aerfála. This is where residents come to relax and where half the important conversations happen — mirroring the bathhouse culture of the outside world.",
          "paths": [
            "garden-path",
            "trade-post"
          ]
        },
        "trade-post": {
          "description": "A covered stall and workshop space where commerce happens. Goods from outside arrive through the Anchor portal and are stored here before distribution. Craft tools line the walls for residents who produce goods for trade. A ledger tracks what comes in and what goes out. When the Anchor is opened for trade visitors, this is where transactions happen — under the Chamberlain's watchful eye and their very particular ideas about fair pricing.",
          "paths": [
            "hot-spring"
          ]
        }
      },
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "factions": [],
      "name": "The Hearth Springs",
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "Heartgate Basin",
      "x": -10,
      "y": 15,
      "basicInfo": "The recreational and commercial district of the Demi-Plane. When recreation or commerce development reaches Growing, the Chamberlain leads the creator here — to hot springs that bubbled up from nothing, gardens that grew because someone wished for beauty, and a trade post ready for the first merchant willing to do business with a pocket dimension. This is where the Demi-Plane starts to feel worth visiting, not just worth building.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The hot spring's water has a subtle restorative effect — not healing, but rejuvenating. Visitors who bathe here recover from fatigue faster and find their mood improved. The Chamberlain attributes this to the Demi-Plane responding to the creator's needs. In truth, the spring is drawing on the ambient mana that the Demi-Plane's infrastructure channels, converting it into a form that benefits living things. If mana infrastructure ever fails, the spring's effects diminish.",
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
