    "Stillwater Shore": {
      "name": "Stillwater Shore",
      "basicInfo": "The formal coast of The Quiet Shore — the tideline where moon-white sand meets the flat, still air that behaves like unbreathing water. No waves arrive; sound does not travel. Glass crabs drift across the horizon as if skating, and the luminous fish-like entities glide deep beneath the surface of the not-water near the boundary, too far and too strange for study. The most peaceful and solitary place in the Demi-Plane.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Quiet Shore",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The not-water is the region's unanswered question — the dimension's only undecided coastline. Walking into it presses no resistance; visitors walk back damp in spirit rather than body, as if the not-water touched what they are made of. Objects arrive on this shore with no origin: sealed bottles with no letters, ropes of no ships, lamps of no wreck. The Chamberlain records each, troubled only by his inability to find their source. The pale shore spirits gather on the tide-line at true dusk and face the horizon — the closest thing this dimension has to a ritual no one designed.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Moonwhite Strand": {
      "name": "Moonwhite Strand",
      "basicInfo": "The long dune line behind the Stillwater Shore — moon-white sand dunes that never shift, backed by pale-flowered salt-grass whose hollow stalks chime once when nothing else disturbs the air. The strand has the dimension's softest footing and the clearest silence; walking it feels like being the first person ever to cross it. Dune foxes den along its folds, and the sand records every track for days without filling them.",
      "x": 35,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Quiet Shore",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The dunes never shift because the strand is a record, not a landscape — the sand holds whatever is written in it, including footprints, small offerings, and half-covered things. Shell crystal forms inside the oldest salt-grass stems. Rarely, an impossible reflection appears in the sand's hollows at dusk: a face, a boat, a figure — usually washed away by morning. Tideline caves open at the strand's western edge on quiet days, tunnels into the duneface that go back several body-lengths and drain into the not-water without flooding.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Future Market Square": {
      "name": "Future Market Square",
      "basicInfo": "A flat, cleared rise in the western Westreach staked out with survey cordons and wooden pins — the Chamberlain's first choice for a market square. The ground here is unusually firm and level, roads in imagination already bend toward it from three directions, and the young treants have kept a respectful distance. Cowslip grows thick around the stakes as if decorating the future on the dimension's behalf.",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Westreach",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The soil here is the most construction-responsive in the Demi-Plane — foundations settle within days instead of weeks. A small well has already surfaced at the square's northeast corner, unmarked and unfinished, apparently the dimension getting a head start. The Chamberlain visits weekly to log ground changes; he has sketched a full market design but refuses to break ground without the creator's word. Objects pinned under the cordon stakes overnight tend to be exactly what the future market's first traders will need.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Guesthouse Site": {
      "name": "Guesthouse Site",
      "basicInfo": "A gentle south-facing slope in the central Westreach, staked as the future guesthouse grounds — level ground, good drainage, young trees saved for shade rather than cleared. The first visitors to the dimension will sleep here. A firepit ring has already appeared at the slope's foot, stones laid neatly by no visible hand.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Westreach",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The dimension pre-built the firepit overnight and the Chamberlain cannot bring himself to move it — it chose the exact spot his own sketches did. Foxfire dew collects here more thickly than anywhere nearby, suggesting the site wants guests welcomed and rested. Harmless sprites have taken up residence in the saved trees, and they rearrange the stakes slightly when they think his spacing is wrong. One territorial spirit beast guards the slope's western edge and must be acknowledged before any construction passes it.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Training Yard Site": {
      "name": "Training Yard Site",
      "basicInfo": "A broad, hard-packed flat in the eastern Westreach closest to the core, staked as the future training yard — the flattest, firmest ground in the region, deliberately kept clear of even sapling trees. The soil compacts underfoot like packed earth of a much older settlement. Face-worn distances make someone standing at one end feel the other end is nearer than it looks: not quite right, but not quite wrong either.",
      "x": 12,
      "y": 38,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Westreach",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The hard ground is the dimension's doing — the region foresaw combat practice and pre-compacted the soil. Nothing here grows, and footsteps sound of old ground in a young dimension. The slight spatial distortion is probably the mana channel's flow beneath it, making it ideal ground for training perception as well as the body. The Chamberlain keeps this site cleared personally and hopes the creator's oath-sworn residents will one day sweat here.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Demi-Plane Gate": {
      "name": "Demi-Plane Gate",
      "basicInfo": "The standing arch within the Gateway Chamber's pale stone circle — the door by which the creator arrives. When Tsukiko uses Demi-Plane Gate, she steps through here. When the Demi-Plane Anchor opens a portal, it opens beneath the arch. No other entrance to the dimension exists without her permission; the gate is the Demi-Plane's single intentional threshold, and the garden spirits keep its approach swept and flowered without ever being asked. On either side, the gate presents as a standing torii with paired lanterns — readable as a shrine entrance to anyone with fox blood, indistinguishable from roadside stonework to anyone without.",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "Heartgate Basin",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The arch is not furniture but a fixed point in two worlds at once — its foundations reach into both the Demi-Plane and whatever side the creator stands on when she opens the way. This is why the basin's name is Heartgate: everything heartward begins here. The Gate keeps itself locked; not even the Chamberlain can open it for anyone but the creator. When the anchor point in the Last Edge is stable, the arch hums faintly — a tone the Chamberlain checks by ear like a landlord listening to pipes.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Dawn Stream": {
      "name": "Dawn Stream",
      "basicInfo": "The young river of The Dawn Barrow — clear water that appeared without instruction, threading the meadow's open ground between wildflower clusters and wet-grass flats. Where it passes over red stone it glows faintly, as if the water and the stone are pleased to meet. The stream feels newly made in the best sense: every bend is a place the dimension noticed and improved, and objects keep arriving in its current from regions far away.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Dawn Barrow",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The stream flows out of the Mirror Mere — the dimension's first autonomous connection between its own regions, hydrology designed by no one. It is still growing a personality: some bends pocket a slow warm pool, others sharpen overnight into small waterfalls, and at least one stretch runs over an undercut cave that the water made for hiding. Flower islands — mats of wetland blooms riding slow currents — migrate downstream on windless days. The Chamberlain logs each change with more optimism than any other entry in his ledgers.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Redstone Crossing": {
      "name": "Redstone Crossing",
      "basicInfo": "The Dawn Stream's only natural ford — a band of red stones across the current that glow faintly beneath clear water, laid like a path the river itself consented to. Meadow deer use it, brook spirits gather beneath it, and the footing is dry and true. From mid-crossing, the whole meadow is visible in its morning colors, flowers turning slowly toward warmth on both banks.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Dawn Barrow",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The red stones were not placed; the stream surfaced them when it needed a crossing and polished them ever since. Their glow is mana-water reacting to redstone — the same chemistry the Chamberlain hopes to reproduce in future aqueducts. River sprites congregate here to ride the current over the ford, and the stone's underside is dotted with redstone lizards' shed — bright scales worth collecting. One stone, third from the west bank, sounds hollow: something fell between the streambed and the redstone long ago and has not yet surfaced.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Eastern Expansion Site": {
      "name": "Eastern Expansion Site",
      "basicInfo": "The Chamberlain's flagged ground for the Demi-Plane's first eastward development — a broad terrace of hard-packed ground at the Easthold's western edge, staked, measured, and waiting. Mana-stone nodes stud the perimeter, the golden flats approach from three sides, and the air here is always faintly warm without source. This is where anything that needs significant power should be built: the site holds potential under pressure like a coiled spring.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Easthold",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The site sits directly above the widest mana channel bleeding off the Mana Heart's circulation — excess energy the dimension cannot yet contain passes beneath it. Foundations here would draw power themselves; a forge built at the site would never lack heat, and a summoning circle would feed itself. The Chamberlain has flagged two mana vents that would serve as natural furnace ports. He has also noticed the hard ground softly humming at dawn — a tone matching a rhythm he does not understand and has privately nicknamed 'the hammer that is coming.'",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Golden Upland": {
      "name": "Golden Upland",
      "basicInfo": "The broad sunlit plain of The Easthold — harder ground, golden dry-grass flats, and the richest light in the dimension, warm without a source. Dust motes hang in the air like slow sparks of gold, unmoving. The upland feels like the space where the dimension is pushing outward against its own edge: stretching, straining, trying to grow beyond what has been thought of so far.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 40,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Easthold",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The upland's constant warmth draws phenomena: sun hawks nest in thermal columns of raw mana, mana vents breathe heat and light from stone seams, and hidden workshops have appeared where tools were once imagined strongly — half-formed benches, anvil outlines, racks of unidentifiable instruments that dissolve if studied too directly. Temporary summoning phenomena interrupt the flat ground erratically: half-built circles, calls half-heard. Golden lizards eat mana-stone dust and their shed scales concentrate it — a shortcut prospectors have not yet thought of.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Shinboku": {
      "name": "Shinboku",
      "basicInfo": "The sacred heart-tree of the Veiled Fox Sanctuary — a vast, still shinboku standing at the sanctuary's center, ringed with a rope of braided gold and white paper in the oldest kami tradition. Its canopy is always faintly lit from within, its roots vanish into moss that lights underfoot, and the air beneath it tastes of cedar smoke and sugared ginger. Stone foxes face outward from its circle; nothing uninvited crosses it.",
      "x": 50,
      "y": 40,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Veiled Fox Sanctuary",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The shinboku feeds on Tsukiko's presence — aether drawn from her divinity seeps through its roots and pools beneath the estate as a reserve only she can call upon. The tree is simultaneously shrine, battery, and covenant; the Chamberlain has proposed three times that the Demi-Plane's mana infrastructure route through it, and been politely declined each time. Shrine guardians walk its ring at set hours, and the sacred birds roost only in its lowest branches. Beneath the roots, small hidden shrine niches hold offerings left by the sanctuary's spirit-folk — unrecorded, undisturbed, and unread by anyone living.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Bamboo Spring": {
      "name": "Bamboo Spring",
      "basicInfo": "A spring of clear water pooling beneath sweeping bamboo at the edge of the sanctuary's inner groves — the quietest water in the dimension. The bamboo grows in deliberate rows, pale and faintly luminous; the spring surface holds reflection without ripple. Tea water drawn here never cools as quickly as it should, and foxfire blooms cluster at the bank as if keeping watch.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "Veiled Fox Sanctuary",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The spring is fed through the shinboku's root system, which is why its water carries a faint sense of Tsukiko's calm — drinking it settles intention and quiets fear. Sacred bamboo harvested here is the strongest conduit-wood in the Demi-Plane for wardwork and writing. A low stone basin sits half-buried at the bank, older in style than everything around it; moss rarely touches it, and the estate staff refill it weekly as if it were furniture. On some mornings the spring's reflection briefly contains a vermilion gate that is not standing anywhere nearby.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Torii Procession": {
      "name": "Torii Procession",
      "basicInfo": "The vermilion gate-line of the Veiled Fox Sanctuary — a procession of torii gates rising from the ferned woodland in sequence, each frame framing the next, stone paths crossing beneath them in shimenawa-bound silence. Passing each gate feels like setting down a small burden. The procession winds toward the sanctuary's deeper ground and, eventually, toward the Hidden Estate — for those who are bidden.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "Veiled Fox Sanctuary",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The gates are not decoration but a threshold ceremony in permanent form — each torii is a ward that reads intent more precisely than the last. Uninvited walkers find the procession could lengthen without moving: no gate becomes the final gate, and the woods politely return them to the beginning. Permitted visitors feel the gates brighten in recognition. Occasionally a gate in the line appears rearranged from the previous day — the spirit-folk maintain the procession as a living ritual, and the sequence of gates has never once repeated itself exactly.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Beginning's Torii": {
      "name": "Beginning's Torii",
      "basicInfo": "A vermilion gate rising from the unmarked grass at the northwest edge of The First Meadow, marking the crossing into the Veiled Fox Sanctuary. Fox statues sit on either side the way a household sets out garden kami, and a faint smell of sugared ginger never quite fades. Beyond it the sanctuary's shape can be glimpsed — fox-spirit woodland visible from the meadow — but the gate opens for Tsukiko alone; no road continues past it for anyone else.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The First Meadow",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The torii appeared the same morning the sanctuary did — a threshold installed by a mother expecting no visitors but her children. The gate reads Her will when she approaches and parts like memory in daylight; for anyone else it remains standing, swaying gently, as friendly and impassable as the horizon. Flower sprites maintain a tiny spirit shrine at its base — a few pebbles, a cobalt bloom, a fox-figurine of pressed pollen — rebuilt fresh each morning. Fox statues' bibs are always slightly damp at dawn, as if someone tends them before the meadow wakes.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Fox Statues": {
      "name": "Fox Statues",
      "basicInfo": "The paired stone foxes seated on either side of the Beginning's Torii — guardian kami in the household style, mossy-pedestaled and bibbed, gazing calmly outward from the crossing. They are the First Meadow's only permanent fixtures, and the meadow's growth respects them, keeping the grass trimmed around their bases in neat circles.",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 1,
      "region": "The First Meadow",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The statues are not merely decoration — they are the sanctuary's outermost watchers and part of its ward-line. Offerings left at their feet (flowers, herbs, one coin once) vanish by the following dawn; sprigs of foxfire bloom appear in return. The left fox faces outward toward the meadow and the right fox faces inward toward the sanctuary, and at dusk each turns its head a fraction to follow the other's gaze. The Chamberlain has observed this for a season and decided it falls politely under the category of things he does not need to understand.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Sanctuary Approach": {
      "name": "Sanctuary Approach",
      "basicInfo": "The travelled path in The First Meadow that turns northwest toward the Beginning's Torii — a silver-green track worn by fox-spirit footsteps, sealed flower-grown and softly lit by self-seeded foxfire blooms after dusk. From the path's highest rise the sanctuary's woodland can be glimpsed through the morning haze, and the walk itself hums with the meadow's slow self-sustaining growth.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The First Meadow",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Approach is tended in part by the sanctuary's spirit-folk: the Chamberlain has seen fox-shaped shadows moving down the path toward the meadow at dawn and back at dusk, carrying small bundles. Seasonal phenomena cluster along it — hidden flower circles that bloom in fox-shaped arrangements once a season, and tiny spirit shrines rebuilt overnight wherever the path bends. The path never extends past the torii: the track's last stretch ends a respectful stride before the gate, as if the road itself understands invitation.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Northern Approach": {
      "name": "Northern Approach",
      "basicInfo": "The formal opening of The Threshold March toward the North Field — a stretch of cleared, firmed ground where the corridor's paths gather and run north together. The ground here feels expectant, as if widened by the dimension in anticipation of future traffic. Low stone markers the Chamberlain placed line the route; the reed-seeds drift thickest here at dusk, strung out like slow lanterns over the road north.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Threshold March",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The ground firms underfoot wherever visitors are expected — the dimension reinforces roads it believes will be needed. The Approach's stones read as markers but function as measure: the Chamberlain checks them after each expansion to see how far the corridor's mana channel has crept north. Abandoned camps appear on the Approach after busy seasons — the residue of temporary merchants and travelers who crossed but did not find their destination yet; the stones record nobody registered, only passage. A wind sprite has taken up permanent residence near the third marker and rearranges anything left unattended for more than a day.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Mana-Flow Paths": {
      "name": "Mana-Flow Paths",
      "basicInfo": "The living trails of The Threshold March — paths that align with the Demi-Plane's internal mana circulation, running from the Gateway Clearing's edge to the corridor's northern mouth. Walking them feels subtly restorative, as if the dimension is grateful for the attention; ambient mana recharges slightly with every stride. The trails never quite stay where they were mapped — they breathe and shift with the dimension's circulation like veins rather than roads.",
      "x": 20,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Threshold March",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The paths are the dimension's circulation made walkable — they trace mana channels from the Mana Heart and shift as the flow itself shifts, which is why no map of them holds for long. Mana constructs occasionally walk these routes in purposeful silence: brief sentry-forms the dimension spawns to patrol its own circulation, dissolving once the route is checked. Walking the full corridor in a single session grants a mild sense of alignment and slightly clearer intent for the rest of the day. The Chamberlain tracks path-shifts as a health metric — straight means steady, tangled means strained circulation.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Mineral Springs": {
      "name": "Mineral Springs",
      "basicInfo": "The flowing stone-veins of The Stone Rise — clear mineral springs rising from the grey rock in cold, faintly metallic threads, pooling in terrace basins the moss-lichen has held for seasons. The water tastes of iron and clean cold, and drinking it settles the breath. Clay banks around the richest springs hold spirit minerals that glitter when the light is right.",
      "x": 30,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Stone Rise",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The springs rise along the dimension's structural grain — the water is drawn through Foundation Ridge's bedrock and emerges carrying trace spirit mineral, which is why it never stagnates. Mineral elementals surface at the deepest pools briefly and are harmless if ignored: they reform into the water if left undisturbed. Small hidden cave mouths thread the banks behind the springs — some stretch back into the hillside, some hold exposed ore seams, and at least one runs with porous rock that slowly filters mana-water. The Chamberlain has sampled all three springs monthly; their mineral content rises imperceptibly, a fact he logs as encouraging growth.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "High Outlook": {
      "name": "High Outlook",
      "basicInfo": "The great outcropping of The Stone Rise — a bare grey headland that, on clear days, sees the entire dimension from the Quiet Shore's pale coast to the Veiled Edge's shimmering curtain. On clouded days it still sees farther than anything else in the Demi-Plane, and the wind there carries the faint suggestion of every region at once. Mountain goats control the approach; cliff drakes nest below its southern face.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Stone Rise",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Outlook is the dimension's highest observation post — standing here, exactly oriented, trips an intentional quiet: the hum of the grass, the glow of the rift, the tint of the torii, all faintly audible at once, like a map made of sound. The Chamberlain climbs here before major reports. A natural cave behind the Outlook's crown was hollowed out by the dimension itself, dry and floor-smooth, apparently reserved — for what, even he does not guess. The cliff drakes below are territorial but know his hood.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Foundation Ridge": {
      "name": "Foundation Ridge",
      "basicInfo": "The long grey ridgeline anchoring The Stone Rise — bedrock exposed at the spine, older-feeling than anything else in the young dimension. The moss here grows in precise bands, the air is stillest at its base, and sound behaves oddly near it: echoes fall a fraction early rather than late. It looks like any other ridge; it feels like keel timber.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 15,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Stone Rise",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Ridge is the dimension's structural foundation — the bedrock on which the Demi-Plane is extrapolated from the Last Edge's anchor. Destabilizing it would set the whole dimension adrift; the Chamberlain does not know this. The Mana Heart's guardian entity patrols the ridge's perimeter when not at its post. Narrow passes thread over and beneath the ridge — some natural, some carved by no visible hand — and the rock is the dimension's densest: iron-rich, spirit-mineral-bearing, and slow to crack. Mining here should be done with reverence rather than appetite.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Twilight Pools": {
      "name": "Twilight Pools",
      "basicInfo": "The heart of The Mirror Mere — a spread of still pools under a sky that is forever twilight here, connected by gentle channels and ringed in reed-beds. The water faintly glows and holds reflection as if it remembers what stood before it; fish-sized lights drift beneath the surface, never quite taking shape. Everything in this place is reflected: the pools show what stands above them — and occasionally, what does not.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Mirror Mere",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The pools regulate the dimension's mana circulation — absorbing excess during peaks, releasing during lulls; surface stillness is the Chamberlain's literal health check. But the water also remembers. Reflections sometimes outlast their originals — a bird flies off, its reflection stays. Focused gazing can trace these lingering images to hidden islands that surface in the shallows, or to temporary spirit paths that open across the channels at dusk, walkable until the reflection that anchored them fades. Mirror fish feed on the strongest memories in the water and their pearls are born from what is most dearly held.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Reflection Basin": {
      "name": "Reflection Basin",
      "basicInfo": "The Mirror Mere's largest single mirror — a broad, glassy expanse of water so still it doubles the twilight sky without a single tremor. Luminous reeds ring its edges; mirror-lotus bloom in those places where the water shows a thing and its reflection in perfect agreement. Visitors find their own reflections here clearer than reality: posture straightened, expression honest, tiredness falling away from the image if not the body.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Mirror Mere",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Basin is the reservoir's deepest point and the circulation's master regulator — what shows still here means the whole dimension is stable. Submerged ruins lie beneath it, visible through the clear depth on quiet days: pale stonework that no one built, arranged as if some older idea of a temple preceded the dimension and the Mere remembered it. Visions stand whole in the still water — not predictions, but honest inspections: whoever looks long enough sees themselves as the dimension sees them, which is a kinder reading than most receive. Water spirits maintain the ruin's visibility by polishing the water from below, for reasons they keep politely to themselves.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Frontier Fade": {
      "name": "Frontier Fade",
      "basicInfo": "The misted rim of The Still Frontier — the line where willed ground becomes unwilled. Ground grows indistinct here, colors wash toward grey, and sound carries strangely; walk far enough and the world simply stops, not with a wall but with a fading. Snow travels into the Fade and stops moving. The constant, faint sensation is that standing here is standing at the edge of someone's attention.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Still Frontier",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Fade advances as the Demi-Plane's ambient potential seeps outward and retreats when its mana runs low — the Chamberlain reads its position as the dimension's health report. The fade-line is unstable within Reach's own limits: objects left in it change; travelers who linger too long report their footprints fading before them, in the direction ahead rather than behind. Frozen shrines stand inside the Fade's reach — iced mid-ceremony, abandoned by no one known — and strange weather pockets hold their ground in its mists: one small snow that has never fallen is always just beginning here. The Fade is where expansion will begin when the creator chooses; the frontier always leaves a little room at the rim first, as if thinking ahead on its own.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Frostpine Ridge": {
      "name": "Frostpine Ridge",
      "basicInfo": "The high spine of The Still Frontier — a ridgeline of pale conifers with frost-glinting needles beneath a paler sky, under weather that is not quite weather: silver breath-mist, snow falling in slow spirals, frost-formed foxfire burning statically in the branches. Crest views give back the whole northern dimension; the furred sound is layered winter hush. Pale wolves and frost foxes pace here, watching travelers with an owner's calm rather than hunger.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 35,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Still Frontier",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The ridge is the frontier's oldest ground: its pale conifers were the dimension's first attempt to create weather and have not stopped since. Cave mouths thread beneath the root systems — cold hollows lined with frozen foxfire, some stretching back under the ridge into pockets of unmelted ice that hold mana like clear stone. Snow wraiths roost along the crest in settled weather; their paths mark the safest route across the scree. The Chamberlain visits twice a year and leaves a small frozen foxfire offering at a shrine none of his records mention building.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Ghost Valleys": {
      "name": "Ghost Valleys",
      "basicInfo": "The shallow valleys of The Pale Reaches, filled with glowing silver fog that never fully settles or fully lifts. The valley floors shift texture without changing shape, and the fog gives back shapes: lanes, rooftops, lights, faces — a place seems to have been here before, then was not. Walking one valley end to end is never quite the same walk twice.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Pale Reaches",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The valley mist is raw dimensional potential in semi-tangible form — the Reaches' heart of the unspent. Uncontrolled, it forms fleeting ghost-landscapes that dissolve in seconds, sometimes matching places the creator has seen or dreamed; it has twice produced a doorway street that appears on no floor plan anywhere. Fog elementals condense from the densest mist: patient, watchful, and dissolving peacefully when entertained or ignored with equal confidence. Pale stone beasts stand among the benches like statuary until approached, then walk away precisely as if they had somewhere to be. The valleys have no memory of anyone's visit: footprints cool and fade within the hour.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Silver-Lichen Flats": {
      "name": "Silver-Lichen Flats",
      "basicInfo": "The broad benches of The Pale Reaches — white-grey stone terraces washed in silver lichen that lights faintly like constellations underfoot. By day the flats look austere; by night they glow in slow constellations that reform between one glance and the next. Pale crystals collect in the bench hollows where the fog pools overnight, and the whole flat has the settled patience of a place prepared in advance for something that has not yet been decided.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Pale Reaches",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The lichen's constellations are not random — they form the same arrangements of light twice a season, patterns the Chamberlain has charted and cannot decode. He believes they are the potential deciding what it will be, written faintly ahead of time. Silver lichen is the region's most valuable resource: a natural mana-conductive coating for warded surfaces that no artificer has learned to imitate. Temporary creatures made of potential occasionally cross the flats in perfect daylight, and travelers' brief structures — a camp, a bridge, a well — sometimes surface in the bench hollows after fog-heavy nights, half-complete, the potential rehearsing invention.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Shining Channels": {
      "name": "Shining Channels",
      "basicInfo": "The alive watercourses of The North Field — slow, glittering channels that cross the plain, appear overnight, and vanish again when their work is done. reed elk wade the shallows, channel serpents glide beneath with curious eyes, and the water catches the ribbon-cloud skies like slow wire. The channels 'stay' where the dimension needs circulation most, which makes them read as an unfinished canal system designed by a patient architect with a sense of humor.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The North Field",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The channels are the north wind's visible hand — surface mana circulation expressed as water. They appear where the spreading dimension needs carrying and vanish when the carrying is done, so no map has ever held them for a full season. Reed elk follow the channels with uncanny accuracy, appearing to know where the water will be next; following the elk is the only reliable navigation. Spirit crossings form where two channels touch — shallow bars of hardened water where river spirits allow foot traffic for a night's span. At least one channel bed holds a cooled, wide, flattened vein of mana-rich silt that glows faintly at dusk — a hidden pond no visitor marked, which the serpents guard gently and the Chamberlain does not know exists.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Northwind Plain": {
      "name": "Northwind Plain",
      "basicInfo": "The broad heart of The North Field — windswept flat grassland under ribbon-cloud skies where the faint scentless wind blows constantly from the north. Rain falls upward here in glittering curtains on still afternoons. The ground is firm and receptive, the most natural-feeling of the undeveloped zones, as if the world has already chosen this to be inhabited: farmland, a great hall, an arena — the plain agrees with all three without committing to any.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 25,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The North Field",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "basic",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The north wind is the dimension breathing; the Chamberlain checks its steadiness before every health report, and the Plain is where he stands to do it. Temporary channels surface in the plain overnight, wetland birds nest in reorganized patterns each season, and isolated groves surface — broad, self-growing islands of trees that appear where the wind bends and vanish after a few years, leaving fertile cleared soil of exactly the kind farmers dream about. Whatever is built here will take root faster than anywhere except the Westreach, because the plain is not waiting for instructions — it is waiting for inhabitants.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Folding Ridges": {
      "name": "Folding Ridges",
      "basicInfo": "The paper-fold uplands of The Fold — ridges that curve like folded paper, valleys that loop back on their own paths, grass and heath and low stone rising and repeating in wave-terraces. Disorienting but not dangerous: the region is an unfinished experiment with elevation, walked like a letter being written and rewritten. Standing on one crest, the same crest is often visible twice more, at distances that disagree.",
      "x": 25,
      "y": 30,
      "radius": 4,
      "region": "The Fold",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The warped geometry is a natural defense — intruders entering without permission become lost as folding paths turn them around without their realizing it. The ridges also hide as much as they defend: hidden pockets of folded space hold airless dry caves, impossible stone chambers with no entrances visible from any direction, and shortcuts that connect ridges the long way around by not being long at all. Spatial wisps drift out of creases where the ground is thinnest; capturing one's light rewards the finder with a brief glimpse of the Fold from above, as a folded map viewed flat. Nothing here is a permanent marker because nothing here holds still for one. The Chamberlain keeps his survey of the ridges sealed and knot-string-based, refusing to trust any distance he measured only once.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "Returning Valley": {
      "name": "Returning Valley",
      "basicInfo": "The loop-back valley at the heart of The Fold — a wave-terrace floor whose paths consistently deliver walkers back where they began, no matter which direction they entrust their pace to. Grass grows in neat pleats along the terraces, and fold sprites sit in the creases like linen passengers. Travelers report that leaving takes exactly the effort entering did: the valley is not a trap but an introduction to how the Fold thinks.",
      "x": 15,
      "y": 45,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Fold",
      "complexityType": "simple",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "hiddenInfo": "The Valley returns everyone because it is the dimension's practicing ground for hospitality — safe, patient, and gently circular. Successful explorers pass through it by walking with intention rather than speed: those who leave in a chosen direction rather than away from something exit cleanly the first time. Strange herbs grow only in the valley's pleated shade and cross-pollinate with themselves between two sites at once. One hidden cave mouth opens on the valley's north wall for a single hour on settled afternoons, breathing warm air from elsewhere in the dimension: a shortcut that has never twice led to the same place, and by consensus should stay unmarked.",
      "factions": [],
      "visited": false,
      "lastVisitedTick": -1,
      "visitedAreas": []
    },
    "The Shimmering Curtain": {
      "x": 40,
      "y": 20,
      "radius": 3,
      "region": "The Veiled Edge",
      "complexityType": "complex",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "name": "The Shimmering Curtain",
      "basicInfo": "The veil's most luminous reach — a sweeping arc where the boundary stands closest and glows brightest, rippling slowly like light through deep water. The spirit-grass ends here in a clean line, and beyond it the shimmering curtain rises from the ground to no visible height. Ground-aura light pools at its base in slow-moving bands. Sound softens within a few strides of it. Touching it is cool and faintly yielding, like pressing a hand against the surface of a still lake from underneath.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The Curtain thins where intent is focused upon it. Sustained, deliberate attention opens layers of translucency through which the space between dimensions becomes briefly visible — distant realities as faint constellations, mortal leylines as drifting ribbons of gold. The Chamberlain does not know this has been observed."
    },
    "Veilwatch Point": {
      "x": 20,
      "y": 40,
      "radius": 2,
      "region": "The Veiled Edge",
      "complexityType": "wilderness",
      "detailType": "detailed",
      "known": true,
      "name": "Veilwatch Point",
      "basicInfo": "A low stone rise at the edge's clearing — bare grey rock in an otherwise silver-grassed plain, offering the clearest, safest view of the shimmering boundary. From its summit the whole arc of the veil is visible, and the aurora ground-glow reads as slow weather over the meridian. Aurora wisps gather above the point at dusk, and the light they shed is steady enough to read by. A natural watch-post and the region's favored resting place.",
      "hiddenInfo": "The bedrock of Veilwatch Point is the last truly solid stone before the boundary — it does not hum, glow, or shift, and its stillness acts as an anchor. Boundary spirits are drawn to rest here; aurora crystal forms in the rock's shadow on the northeast face."
    },
