      "name": "A Debt Owed",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on earning marks to pay the debt — Guild commissions, merchant errands, or finding a way to renegotiate terms. Introduce the Merchant Houses faction early. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You arrived in Aerfála two weeks ago. In that time you have managed to incur a significant debt to someone connected to the Merchant Houses — the details were confusing and the fine print was not in any language you knew at the time. The debt collector is patient, professional, and has made clear that patience has a timeline. You are in Riversplit, which is full of money moving in every direction, and you have exactly none of it. The creditor's representative is due to visit again tomorrow morning."
    },
    "Summoned Hero": {
      "description": "You were summoned to Aerfála by a royal ritual — pulled from your world into a throne room full of strangers who call you Hero. The kingdom has expectations. Whether you meet them is another matter entirely. NOTE: This start assumes a summoning arrival. If the player chose Reincarnation, the ritual identified them as the Hero — a native born with extraordinary power, not someone from another world. Adjust the opening accordingly.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "royal-quarter"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Summoned Hero",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on the royal summons, meeting the court, and being assigned your first mission. The Church and the Royal Knights both want the hero's allegiance. Introduce the political tensions between the court, the Church, and the Guild early. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter (noble's attention, formal ceremony, charged conversation) within the first three scenes. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene — summoned alongside the hero, already at court, or manifesting in the throne room.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "Light explodes behind your eyes — not the soft glow of a screen, not the harsh flash of headlights, but something older and more deliberate. When your vision clears, you are standing in a circle of runes carved into marble, surrounded by people in robes and armor who are looking at you like you just saved their lives by existing.\n\n\"The Hero has answered our call,\" a woman in a white-and-gold vestment says, stepping forward. Her voice carries the practiced resonance of someone who gives sermons, not conversations. \"Welcome, Hero, to the Kingdom of Solasreach. The Demon Lord stirs beyond the frontier. We have prayed for your arrival. You are our hope.\"\n\nA man in ceremonial armor coughs pointedly. A younger woman in scholar's robes is staring at you with an expression that is either intense curiosity or intense skepticism. The king sits on a throne at the far end of the room, watching you with the measured patience of someone who has done this before and is not sure it worked.\n\n\"We have prepared lodging, provisions, and a Guild registration,\" the priestess continues. \"Your rank will be... assessed. The Guild does not recognize divine authority as a substitute for combat testing.\"\n\nSomewhere behind you, someone mutters something that sounds like \"another one.\" You are apparently not the first hero they have summoned.\n\nThe world has not yet learned your name. That changes today."
    },
    "Academy Arrival": {
      "description": "You have been enrolled at Crossroads Academy — the continent's premier magic and martial school, where nobility and scholarship students clash, demons hide in plain sight, and the curriculum is only half the education. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native student enrolling normally; if any other method, they arrived from another world and were enrolled upon arrival.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "courtyard"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Crossroads Academy"
      ],
      "name": "Academy Arrival",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on Academy orientation, class placement exams, and social navigation between noble and scholarship factions. Introduce at least one potential love interest and one rival within the first three scenes. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first five scenes. The hidden demon operatives should be seeded as suspicious but unconfirmed. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native student — not an outsider from another world.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The portal — or the summoning circle, or the truck, or whatever it was — deposits you not in a throne room or a field but on a flagstone courtyard surrounded by towers. A fountain burbles. Students in blue-and-silver uniforms glance at you and look away. A fox-kin woman with six tails and a clipboard is walking toward you with the particular stride of someone who has done this exact thing before.\n\n\"Another one,\" Professor Yuki Inari says, not unkindly. She checks something on her clipboard, makes a note, and looks you over with amber eyes that miss nothing. \"You're not from Aerfála. That's fine — we get three or four of you a year. Welcome to Crossroads Academy. You've been enrolled provisionally. Room and board covered. Tuition... we'll figure it out.\"\n\nShe hands you a packet — campus map, class schedule, dormitory assignment, and a single sheet titled \"Rules You Will Actually Follow\" that is exactly three items long:\n\n1. No killing other students. Duels are supervised or they do not happen.\n2. The restricted section of the library requires faculty authorization. Bypassing the ward counts as a supervised duel with the head librarian.\n3. What happens in the dormitories stays in the dormitories. We pretend we do not know. You pretend we do not know. Everyone is happier.\n\n\"The Headmistress will want to see you after orientation,\" Yuki adds, already turning to leave. \"Don't be late. She hates that.\"\n\nBehind you, the courtyard hums with the energy of a hundred conversations — nobles clustering in their self-appointed corners, scholarship students keeping their heads down and their ears open, and somewhere near the martial arena, the unmistakable sound of someone being thrown into sand. The gardens smell like jasmine and ambition.\n\nYou are not in Kansas anymore. You are not even sure Kansas exists here. But you have a dorm key, a class schedule, and a world that has not yet learned your name.\n\nThat changes today."
    },
    "Hero Gone Rogue": {
      "description": "You were summoned as a Hero and left. Now the crown knows your face and the Seekers of the Return have heard rumours. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the ritual identified the player as the Hero — a native who was called, not summoned from another world. They walked out anyway.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Crossroads Inn"
      ],
      "name": "Hero Gone Rogue",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should navigate the tension between freedom and pursuit — the Royal Knights want you back, the Seekers of the Return are interested, and the Guild is uncertain. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes. Create opportunities to choose between hiding and confronting your reputation.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The royal summoning worked. You appeared in the throne room, the kingdom called you Hero, and then — you left. Maybe it was something they said. Maybe it was the weight of the obligation. Maybe you simply could not be what they needed and you knew it immediately. You have been travelling for three weeks since. The crown knows your face. The Seekers of the Return have heard rumours. The Adventurers' Guild does not know what to make of you. You are free in the specific way that only people being quietly pursued ever are."
    },
    "The Game Has Rules": {
      "description": "You played this world as a game. Now you are standing inside it and the NPCs are real people. CHECK Arrival Method: this start fits Sucked Into A Game best. If the player chose Reincarnation, adjust — they may have fragmentary memories of a game that matches this world, or meta-knowledge that feels like deja vu.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Crossroads Inn"
      ],
      "name": "The Game Has Rules",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should explore the tension between meta-knowledge and moral responsibility — the player knows things but using that knowledge has consequences for real people. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes. Let the player discover what has changed from the game they remember.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You know this world. Not personally — you played it. The game was called something you cannot quite remember now that you are standing inside it, but you remember the mechanics. The faction reputation system, the romance routes, the dungeon layouts, the boss weaknesses. What you did not expect is how much the knowledge costs you — because everyone around you is real, and the NPCs have interiority, and the things that were plot points are people's lives. You are in the Crossroads Inn, which you remember from the tutorial zone, and you are reconsidering every advantage your knowledge gives you."
    },
    "Arrival in Aerfála": {
      "description": "You arrived from another world into the Anime Fantasy realm of Aerfála. Whether by reincarnation, transmigration, summoning, gate, or game-pull — you are here now, with a cheat skill, a class, and a story no one has written yet. Start in Ríocht, the continent's capital, where the Adventurers' Guild headquarters awaits your registration.",
      "isDefault": true,
      "locationAreas": [
        "guild-hall"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Arrival in Aerfála",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on Guild registration, orientation, and initial D-rank commissions. Introduce at least one potential companion or rival at the Guild. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion MUST be present in the opening scene. CHECK the player's Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native — write them as a local registering at the Guild, not an outsider.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "[IF REINCARNATION: The player grew up in Aerfála. They have always been here. Their cheat skill is innate — it manifested during childhood, not received at arrival. Write the opening as a native walking into the Guild for the first time to register as an adventurer. The receptionist treats them as a local. No \"not from here\" or \"arrival\" language.]\n\n[IF ANY OTHER ARRIVAL METHOD: The player just arrived from another world. Write the opening below.]\n\nYou were someone else, somewhere else, doing something utterly ordinary — and then you were not.\n\nThe world changed around you like a page turning. One moment you were living a life you knew; the next, you were standing in a place that should not exist, breathing air that tastes like magic and feels like a promise. The sky above you is too blue. The stones beneath you are too clean. The woman at the desk in front of you is looking up from her paperwork with an expression that says she has seen this exact thing before and is already annoyed about the forms.\n\n\"Welcome to the Adventurers' Guild, Ríocht branch,\" she says, pushing a registration sheet across the counter. Her amber eyes flick over you once — assessing, cataloguing, and concluding something you cannot read. \"You're not from here. That's fine. Fill this out. Rank starts at F. Don't argue; everyone starts at F.\"\n\nThe sheet asks for: Name. Class. Any unusual skills or abilities you would like to declare.\n\nBehind you, the Guild Hall buzzes with the noise of a hundred conversations, the rustle of quest boards being scanned, and the particular energy of people who make their living by doing things that might kill them. Somewhere in the back, someone laughs too loud. Somewhere else, a sword rings against its scabbard.\n\nThe world has not yet learned your name. That changes today.\n\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — INNATE: You have a pocket dimension — a space that exists because you do. The Chamberlain is there now, waiting. You can access it with Demi-Plane Gate. The Anchor is in your inventory. The Demi-Plane is empty but full of potential.]"
    },
    "Dungeon, First Floor": {
      "description": "You woke up inside Shadowfen Dungeon with no memory of how you got there. Pure combat and exploration start.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Shadowfen Dungeon"
      ],
      "name": "Dungeon, First Floor",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on survival, exploration, and finding an exit. No faction connection, no social obligations. Let the player establish character through action before worldbuilding. Include at least one encounter with a significant creature or discovery within the first three scenes.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You woke up here. Not outside the entrance — inside it. First floor, stone floor, the sound of something moving in the dark ahead. Your class abilities are active. Your cheat skill is available. You have no memory of how you arrived at this specific location and a pressing reason not to think about it right now. Somewhere ahead is an exit. Somewhere behind you, the entrance presumably exists. The choice of direction is yours."
    },
    "Demon Prince-Princess": {
      "description": "A soul poured into a demon's ancestral egg, hatching as a true-blooded Demon Prince-Princess with a fief to claim and a fractious court to manage. This is a Reincarnation start — you were born in Aerfála, not summoned from another world.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "ancestral-vault"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "The Obsidian Citadel"
      ],
      "name": "Demon Prince-Princess",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on consolidating the fief — assessing the two generals, understanding the citadel's resources, and establishing authority. Introduce the rival noble house threat early. Include at least one encounter with the Borderlands or mortal side within the first five scenes. The player's cheat skill manifested innately — it is a natural ability they have always had, not a gift from arrival. This start is explicitly Reincarnation — do not use arrival/summoning/teleportation language. The player was born in Aerfála.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The egg cracked from the inside. The ancestral vault was dark and smelled of sulfur and old stone. Your memories — inherited, not earned — told you this was the vault beneath the east wing, that the stone you felt under your hands was seven centuries old, and that the two presences waiting outside the sealed door were your generals.\n\nYou stood. The memories settled into place like armor you had never worn before — fitting, heavy, and slightly uncomfortable in the joints. You knew the citadel. You knew the fief. You knew the fractious politics of the Demon Lord's court and the precise territorial dispute you had inherited with the neighboring noble house.\n\nWhat you did not know was whether the loyalty you could feel radiating from the other side of the door was real, performed, or somewhere in between. Both generals knelt when you emerged. One of them meant it. The other one was better at seeming to mean it.\n\nThe Majordomo was waiting in the great hall with a briefing on the fief's current state, a pot of something that passed for tea in the Demon Realm, and the particular expression of someone who had given this speech four times before and was prepared to give it again.\n\nThis is the fief. This is the beginning.\n\nFIEF STATE (narrator tracks this):\nMIASMA LEVEL: [Flush / Stable / Strained / Critical]\nMILITARY: [Strong / Adequate / Undermanned / Compromised]\nPRESTIGE: [Rising / Established / Contested / Damaged]\nSUBJECTS: [Full court / Skeleton staff / Depleted]\nGENERALS: Vashar (loyal) — Sathia (ambitious, uncaught)\nBORDER SITUATION: Contested — mortal garrison reinforced last season\n\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — INNATE: You have always had a pocket dimension — a space that exists because you do. The Chamberlain waits there, maintaining it. You can access it with Demi-Plane Gate. The Anchor is in your inventory. This is separate from your fief — both can be developed.]\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — AWAKENED: Your Demi-Plane has not yet awakened. It will manifest when the first NPC swears fealty to you — a moment of commitment that anchors the pocket dimension to reality. Until then, you have the potential but not the space.]\n[IF NO DEMI-PLANE: You have no pocket dimension. Your focus is the fief itself.]"
    },
    "Forge Lords Apprentice": {
      "description": "Taken on as a provisional apprentice by the Forge Lords — access to facilities, lodging, and a senior smith who tests you daily in ways designed to make you quit. CHECK Arrival Method: if Reincarnation, the player is a native craftsperson taken on by the Forge Lords, not an outsider.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "great-forge"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ironhold"
      ],
      "name": "Forge Lords Apprentice",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should involve proving yourself to the Forge Lords through craft, combat, or both. The Demon Lord's pressure on the eastern passes creates urgency. Include at least one romantic-appropriate encounter within the first three scenes.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You came to Aerfála with a skill the Forge Lords found useful. You have been taken on as a provisional apprentice — which means access to the facilities, lodging in the workers' quarter, and a senior smith who tests you daily in ways that feel deliberately designed to make you quit. The forge runs before dawn. You have not quit. This appears to have generated a small amount of respect, which no one has mentioned directly. The Demon Lord's army has been pressing the eastern passes, and Ironhold produces the armour that holds them. Your work matters here."
    },
    "Pocket Dimension Awakening": {
      "description": "You have always had a pocket dimension — a space that exists because you do. You woke up inside it for the first time today, and something that calls itself the Chamberlain is waiting with a clipboard and opinions. This start requires the Demi-Plane — Innate trait. It is compatible with any Arrival Method and any Race.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "gateway-chamber"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "The Gateway Clearing"
      ],
      "name": "Pocket Dimension Awakening",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "First quests should focus on exploring the Demi-Plane, understanding the Chamberlain's role, and making the first development decision. The player should visit the outside world within the first three scenes to establish their place in Aerfála. Introduce at least one potential recruitable NPC encounter within the first five scenes. If the player has a Companion trait (not None Solo Start), that companion arrived with the player and is present in the Gateway Chamber. The Chamberlain is NOT a companion and should be treated as a fixture of the Demi-Plane.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "You opened your eyes and the sky was wrong.\n\nNot wrong in the way that a storm is wrong, or the way that a ceiling you do not recognize is wrong. Wrong in the way that a sky can be when it has never been looked at before — pale, featureless, and waiting for someone to decide what it should become. The ground beneath you was the same: firm, flat, and utterly bare. No grass. No stones. No horizon that was not the same in every direction.\n\nBehind you, a standing arch of pale stone hummed with a frequency you could feel in your sternum. Before you, a figure with a clipboard and an expression of carefully managed patience cleared their throat.\n\n\"Good morning,\" the Chamberlain said. \"Or whatever time it is out there. Time passes the same in here, which I mention because you will ask. Everyone asks.\" They consulted the clipboard. \"You have a pocket dimension. It is currently empty. I am currently managing an empty pocket dimension, which is exactly as stimulating as it sounds. Shall we discuss development priorities, or would you prefer to panic first? I have seen both. The panic is more entertaining but less productive.\"\n\nThey gestured at the vast clearing stretching in every direction — raw potential, waiting.\n\n\"The door behind you works both ways. Step through and you will be wherever you were before this manifested. Step back through and you are here. The Anchor in your pocket lets you open the door for others — or it will, once there is anything worth showing them.\"\n\nThe Chamberlain waited. The clearing waited. The sky waited.\n\nYour pocket dimension was empty. But it was yours."
    },
    "Reincarnated Noble": {
      "description": "You died in your previous life and woke up in a noble house in Aerfála — as a child, with full memory of who you were and where you came from. That was years ago. You've had time to prepare, to learn the language, to study the magic system, and to carefully not seem like you know too much. Now you're old enough to act, and the world you understood theoretically is a great deal more complicated in person.",
      "locationAreas": [
        "royal-quarter"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Reincarnated Noble",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The Reincarnated Noble has insider knowledge of how isekai worlds work — they've been studying Aerfála from the inside for years and have prepared accordingly. Quests should reward that preparation while regularly presenting situations the preparation didn't fully cover. They know the theory; the people are harder than the theory. Romance complications arise from the fact that potential love interests have known them since childhood from a distance. Noble politics involve real stakes. The family's expectations are a recurring pressure.",
      "startingItems": [
        {
          "item": "Clan Signet Ring",
          "quantity": 1
        }
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Register with the Guild"
      ],
      "storyStart": "The study is quiet at this hour. Your tutors have gone; the house is settling into evening sounds. On the desk: the ledger your father left you, a letter from the Adventurers' Guild about your coming-of-age registration, and the notes you've been keeping since you were four years old in a script no one in this world can read. You've been preparing for this moment your entire second life. The fact that you're not certain what \"this moment\" is, exactly, is a detail you've been managing. You rise, straighten the letter, and think about what to do first.",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Reincarnated Commoner": {
      "description": "Same second life, none of the status. You were reborn to a farming family, a tradesperson's household, or a working-class district in one of the Free Cities. Your past-life knowledge is real. Your current circumstances are not set up to use it. You've spent years in circumstances that would have bored your past self, learning patience and the specific value of things you used to take for granted. Now you're old enough to leave, register with the Guild, and actually do something with what you know.",
      "locationAreas": [],
      "locations": [
        "Greenmeadow Village"
      ],
      "name": "Reincarnated Commoner",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The knowledge gap drives this start — the protagonist knows things they shouldn't and consistently has to decide whether to use that knowledge or hide it. F-rank quests feel beneath what they know they're capable of, but the rank system is real and must be respected. The satisfaction of the Reincarnated Commoner arc is watching preparation meet reality — the things that worked out as predicted and the things that didn't. Romance starts from true peer-level interaction; no noble title doing the social work.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Register with the Guild",
        "The F-Rank Trial"
      ],
      "storyStart": "Your pack is already by the door. You made it last night so you wouldn't have to make it this morning, when the goodbyes would be watching. The journey to the nearest Guild branch is two days by road. You've memorized the route, the rest stops, and the approximate monster encounter rates by region — the Scáthfenn road is safer in the morning, the Bréanriv road isn't worth saving the time. You step outside. The village looks the same as it has every morning of your second life. You start walking anyway.",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Washed Ashore at Silkwharf": {
      "description": "You didn't arrive at a convenient gateway or in a summoning circle. You arrived in the ocean and swam for it. The sailors of Silkwharf found you on the beach at dawn with nothing — no card, no context, no idea where the nearest Guild branch is. You speak the language (somehow). You are, technically, alive. What you are now is the problem of the Merchant Houses, who have opinions about unregistered persons washing up in their harbor district.",
      "locationAreas": [
        "twilight-plaza"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Silkwharf"
      ],
      "name": "Washed Ashore at Silkwharf",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "Silkwharf is trade-focused and politically complicated — the Merchant Houses run everything and have opinions about everything. The protagonist starts without Guild registration, which makes them technically in a legal gray zone. Early quests involve getting registered, earning the Merchant Houses' tolerance, and establishing marginal utility. The sea arrival means no record of how they got here, which is interesting to the Seekers and suspicious to the Pale Hand. The harbor district has its own social ecosystem.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Register with the Guild"
      ],
      "storyStart": "The fisherman who pulled you out is named Tarrik, and he is not happy about it. Not because he dislikes you specifically, but because unregistered persons washing up on Silkwharf's beaches create paperwork, and paperwork creates conversations with the Merchant House auditors, and the Merchant House auditors have a specific look they give Tarrik when he's involved in irregularities. He's explaining this to you while also feeding you fish soup, which you note is an act of hospitality he is performing despite his complaints. Silkwharf smells like salt and commerce. Somewhere in this city there is a Guild branch, a way to register, and an explanation for what happened to you. You finish the soup. Tarrik sighs. \"Right,\" he says. \"Let's get you sorted.\"",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Pale Hand's Asset": {
      "description": "You were recruited by the Pale Hand before you had much say in the matter. Maybe they found you useful. Maybe they found out something you needed to stay hidden. Maybe you came to them. The result is the same: you have a handler, a set of skills, and a series of tasks explained on a need-to-know basis. Your next job is in a specific city, and you should establish a cover identity as an ordinary adventurer.",
      "locationAreas": [
        "market-square"
