      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Register with the Guild"
      ],
      "storyStart": "The letter dissolves after you read it. That is a feature, not a failure — the Pale Hand's correspondence is designed that way. The briefing is short: the target city, a contact phrase, a description of the cover you're expected to maintain. The Guild registration is already handled; an Adventurer's Card is waiting at the branch under a name that is yours now, legally, for purposes of this assignment. Your handler's instructions were specific: blend in, pursue the stated objective, report through the usual channel. The part they didn't write down, because they never write the obvious things down: don't get interesting. You are in Ríocht. You are an adventurer now, ostensibly. The contact phrase is already memorized. You step into the crowd and become ordinary.",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Demon Realm First": {
      "description": "The isekai transit went wrong — or right, depending on who you ask. Instead of arriving in the human kingdoms, you arrived in the Demon Realm. You do not speak the language (yet — the translation effect is slower here, incomplete). You are in demon territory with no Guild card, no context, and the immediate problem of being very obviously not a demon.",
      "locationAreas": [
        "market-square"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "The Demon Market"
      ],
      "name": "Demon Realm First",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The early arc is survival and communication — learning the language (partial comprehension within five scenes, full fluency by the tenth), navigating demon social customs without violating the Seven Virtues (Pride, Wrath, Lust, Gluttony, Greed, Envy, Sloth — cultural values here, not sins), and determining whether to stay, escape, or engage. Demon society's hierarchy means the protagonist's status is genuinely undetermined — interesting to demons who don't know what category to put them in. The human kingdoms are accessible eventually; the protagonist may not want to leave.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The sky is purple-black in a way that has nothing to do with the time of day. The air carries something — not unpleasant, but present, the way humidity is present, except it isn't humidity. You will learn later that it's miasma, and that you've been breathing it for the last hour without dissolving, which the demon who found you hiding behind what appears to be a market stall has noted with visible interest. She hasn't called for anyone. She's studying you. The stall's owner — who has far too many arms to be human — is watching from a careful distance. The market continues around you in a language that sounds almost like something you recognize, not quite. The demon who found you says something. You don't understand it. She says something else, slower. You don't understand that either. She sighs, which is apparently a universal gesture, and points at herself. \"Kael,\" she says. Or something like it. It might be a name. You point at yourself and tell her yours. She nods. This is, apparently, a start.",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Warden Initiate": {
      "description": "You have been taken in by the Warden Circle — the elven guardians of Caelvir's borders and ancient pacts. Whether you sought them out, were found by them, or arrived in the forest with no better option, they have determined that you have some quality worth cultivating. The training is rigorous. The forest is watching.",
      "locationAreas": [
        "warden-stockade"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Deepbough"
      ],
      "name": "Warden Initiate",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "Warden quests involve the forest — maintaining ancient pacts, investigating border disruptions, dealing with incursions the Circle handles quietly. Early quests are supervised; the protagonist earns increasing autonomy. Romantic options in Caelvir are specific to the elven cultural register — long timelines, formality, and the weight of decisions that elves make knowing they'll outlive most consequences. The political relationship between Caelvir and the other great powers becomes relevant as the protagonist integrates.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The forest at Caelvir's edge is not quiet. That's the first thing you understood. You were expecting quiet — forests in stories are quiet, contemplative, serene. Caelvir is active in every direction: insects, birds, wind in a specific register, the sound of something large moving at a respectful distance. Lady Mirevh of the Canopy is waiting at the treeline. She is taller than you expected and has the expression of someone who has assessed you and reached a provisional conclusion. \"You have instincts,\" she says, without preamble. \"Whether they're useful instincts remains to be demonstrated.\" She turns back into the forest. \"Keep up. The forest will watch how you move.\" You move.",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "Seekers' New Recruit": {
      "description": "The Seekers of the Return — the fellowship of scholars, mages, and displaced persons who study the isekai phenomenon — found you before the Guild did. You are now a research subject who has been offered a position as a research contributor, which comes with a salary, a room in their Ríocht tower, and access to a library with arrival records going back decades.",
      "locationAreas": [
        "seekers-tower"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Seekers' New Recruit",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The Seekers' quests involve research — investigating arrival sites, interviewing displaced persons, recovering relevant artifacts. The protagonist is both researcher and subject. The Seekers' Confession quest is available earlier in this start. The long arc involves the Seekers' failed portal experiment and what came through. The Pale Hand is interested in the Seekers' research. The Demon Lord's Army is more interested than that.",
      "startingItems": [
        {
          "item": "Scholar's Codex",
          "quantity": 1
        }
      ],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [
        "Register with the Guild"
      ],
      "storyStart": "Archivist Loris gives you the tour with the brisk efficiency of someone who has given this tour many times and trusts that you are intelligent enough to keep up. The Seekers' Ríocht tower is three floors of organized research, one floor of organized chaos, and a basement the Archivist mentions once and does not mention again. Your room is small and comfortable. The library card is already in your name. The research agreement you signed has twelve clauses, most of which are reasonable, one of which you intend to ask about later. \"The others will want to interview you,\" Loris says. \"Take a day to settle. Then we begin.\" They leave. The library card sits on the desk. The archive is one floor down. You haven't taken your coat off yet.",
      "isDefault": false
    },
    "The Sword Saint Arrives": {
      "description": "You are already at the end of one story and the beginning of another. The isekai transit brought across a fully-realized Sword Saint — someone who had spent lifetimes mastering a blade to the point where the world itself registered the achievement. You arrived in Aerfála not as a beginner but as a completed thing, stepping into a new context. The question is not whether you can handle what Aerfála offers. The question is what, in Aerfála, is actually worth doing.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "guild-hall"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "The Sword Saint Arrives",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The central challenge for a Sword Saint start is not combat — that is solved — but purpose and connection. Quests should regularly present the gap between the protagonist's capability and the available opposition, and fill it with complications that a sword cannot resolve: political problems, relationship investments, mysteries that require patience rather than force, and eventually opponents who represent genuine challenges from the mythic tier. The narrator should introduce at least one antagonist who registers the protagonist's true level and treats the game of matching it as its own story. Romance is complicated by power differential and simplified by the Sword Saint's specific quality of genuinely seeing people rather than threats.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The Guild registration form asks about your rank. You leave it at A because S feels like a conversation you don't need to have on the first day, and the rank above that doesn't have a box. The receptionist — young, competent, slightly overwhelmed by the queue — processes the form without looking up. When she does look up, something in your bearing makes her pause in a way she doesn't understand and doesn't comment on.\n\nThe card she hands you is standard-issue. You look at it. In your last world you had no use for credentials. Here, apparently, you start at A. That's fine. Aerfála will calibrate on its own timeline.\n\nYou fold the card into your jacket, step back into the Ríocht afternoon, and start deciding what you're actually here for.\n\n[IF COMPANION TRAIT SELECTED (not None Solo Start): Your companion is already here — they arrived with you, found you at the Guild steps, or was waiting in the queue behind you. They have been watching you navigate this mundane interaction with the specific expression of someone who has seen what you can do and finds it deeply amusing that you are filling out paperwork.]"
    },
    "Awakening Blood": {
      "description": "You arrived in Aerfála as something — a standard reincarnation, a hero summoning, any ordinary beginning — and something about the arrival triggered a dormant inheritance you did not know you carried. Your divine blood, demi-god heritage, or mythic lineage activated under the stress of transit. You are in the process of figuring out what you are, in a world that has categories for things like you and strong opinions about those categories.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "royal-quarter"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "Awakening Blood",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The early arc is understanding — what the protagonist's divine nature means, what it wants from them, and what it does to their relationship with the world around them. The goddess is an ongoing contact who provides information in the measure that the protagonist demonstrates they can handle it. Kingdom-level politics surround the summoned hero role; the divine inheritance complicates that role in ways the court is not equipped for. Romantic options include NPCs who respond to the divine nature specifically — who find it clarifying rather than overwhelming — and NPCs who specifically can't tell, which is interesting in a different way.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The goddess who greeted the Hero Summoning did not expect what she found. She masked it quickly — the court didn't notice — but you noticed, because the moment she looked at you something in your blood recognized something in her, and neither of you said anything about it in front of the assembled court.\n\nYou are now, officially, the summoned hero of the Kingdom of Solasreach. You also have a private meeting with the goddess at dawn, which she arranged with the specific efficiency of someone who has dealt with awkward situations for a very long time and has decided to address this one before it becomes a problem. You don't know what she's going to say. You have some hypotheses.\n\nThe court is still celebrating the successful summoning. The knights are measuring you for armor you may or may not need. The Church delegation is preparing a blessing that will either confirm or complicate everything. And somewhere in the back of your mind, something that has been asleep for your entire life is waking up, and it has opinions about the blessing.\n\n[IF DEMI-GOD RACE: Your divine inheritance is stirring — a dormant lineage activated by the stress of arrival. The goddess recognized it. You are about to learn what it means.]\n[IF GOD (INCARNATE) RACE: You chose this form. Or it was chosen for you. Either way, the goddess looked at you and saw something she was not expecting to find in a mortal summoning circle. The private meeting at dawn is going to be considerably more interesting than either of you anticipated.]\n[IF COMPANION TRAIT SELECTED (not None Solo Start): Your companion is present at court — summoned alongside you, already in the throne room, or manifesting in a way the court is not prepared to explain. They felt the moment your blood changed. They are the only person in the room who isn't pretending it didn't happen.]"
    },
    "Demon Lord in Transit": {
      "description": "You were the Demon Lord. You are currently between that title and wherever you are going next — reincarnated after a defeat, deliberately taking mortal form for reasons you have not fully articulated, or woken from a long dormancy into a world that has moved on without you. The Demon Lord's Army you built is still out there. The current Demon Lord, if there is one, occupies a throne that was yours. The human kingdoms have been fighting a war against a version of you that is now complicated.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "market-square"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "The Demon Market"
      ],
      "name": "Demon Lord in Transit",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The Demon Lord start is a political thriller as much as a power fantasy. The protagonist is the most powerful entity in most rooms they enter — the interesting questions are whose rooms they choose to enter, and what they want from the world they find themselves in. The Demon Lord's Army requires management: factions within it will attempt to use the protagonist's return for their own purposes, and navigating which factions to reward and which to discipline is a recurring story texture. The human kingdoms' war effort is aimed at the Army the protagonist built — deciding what to do about that is the long arc. Romance across the line between demon and mortal is the most fraught and most specific kind of connection available in this setting.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The market in the lower Demon Realm is exactly as you remember it and slightly wrong in every detail that matters. The vendor who used to be at this corner is gone. The miasma concentration is lower than it was in your time — someone has been managing it, or something has disrupted the usual source.\n\nThe demon woman who has been watching you since you arrived at this market is not someone you recognize, which means she rose to whatever prominence she carries after your dormancy began. She is good enough at watching that you're reasonably confident she doesn't know you've noticed. You let her watch. It's useful to understand what the current landscape considers worth observing before you decide what to do about your own position in it.\n\nSomewhere in this market, someone knows who you are. Somewhere in the citadel, someone is comparing the miasma signature that just appeared to the records of every Demon Lord who has ever held the throne. The match will not take long.\n\n[IF COMPANION TRAIT SELECTED (not None Solo Start): Your companion is in the market — they followed you here, they were already here, or they found you the way all significant things in the Demon Realm find each other. They understand exactly what you are, and they are here anyway.]\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — INNATE: The Chamberlain is maintaining your Demi-Plane. The Anchor is in your inventory. You can access it with Demi-Plane Gate. This is separate from the Army — a private space that even the Demon Lord's chain of command cannot reach.]\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — AWAKENED: Your Demi-Plane has not yet awakened. It will manifest when the first NPC swears fealty to you — a moment of commitment that anchors the pocket dimension to reality.]"
    },
    "The God Who Stepped Down": {
      "description": "You came here deliberately. The reasons are your own — curiosity about mortal experience, a wager with another deity, a specific person you wanted to meet without the weight of omniscience flattening every interaction, or a situation in Aerfála that required presence you could not provide from outside it. You sealed most of your own power before arriving. The mortal form you inhabit is stable and has a history that the locals accept without question.",
      "isDefault": false,
      "locationAreas": [
        "market-square"
      ],
      "locations": [
        "Ríocht"
      ],
      "name": "The God Who Stepped Down",
      "questGenerationGuidance": "The God Incarnate's central tension is restraint — the power exists, the choice to not use it is ongoing, and the moments when something breaks through the seal are significant. Quests that tempt the use of full power are more interesting than quests that simply require it. The protagonist's genuine ignorance of certain mortal experiences (fatigue, fear, hunger, love as mortals experience it) is a source of both comedy and genuine discovery. Other divine entities appear with increasing frequency as the protagonist's presence registers on the divine network. The seal on the protagonist's power has a specific condition under which it weakens — the narrator establishes this once and uses it consistently as the run's escalation mechanism.",
      "startingItems": [],
      "startingPartyNPCs": [],
      "startingQuests": [],
      "storyStart": "The rain in Ríocht is different from rain anywhere else you have been, which is everywhere. It has the specific texture of a city that has been rained on for centuries and has developed opinions about it. You stand outside the Adventurers' Guild with the registration form you've been holding for approximately ten minutes, not because you're unsure how to fill it out, but because you're deciding what name to write.\n\nThe name you carried before you arrived would mean nothing here. The name of the mortal body you're currently in is real and has a history you've read. The name you actually go by, in the places where names like yours are known, is not available to write on a Guild form.\n\nYou write something reasonable. You go inside.\n\nSomewhere above the rain, something that was watching you make that decision nods with what would be satisfaction if it were capable of satisfaction. It is not capable of satisfaction. It nods anyway.\n\n[IF COMPANION TRAIT SELECTED (not None Solo Start): Your companion is already in the Guild — they know what you are, or they do not and are simply drawn to you for reasons they cannot articulate. Either way, they are the only person in the room who looks at you and sees something other than a new adventurer filling out paperwork.]\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — INNATE: The Chamberlain is maintaining your Demi-Plane. You sealed it along with most of your power — it exists but you cannot access it until the seal weakens. The Anchor is dormant in your inventory.]\n[IF DEMI-PLANE — AWAKENED: Your Demi-Plane has not yet awakened. Even a sealed god may develop a pocket dimension — but only when something in this world earns enough of your commitment to anchor it.]"
    },
