    "generateNPCIntents": {
      "core_principles": "# NPC Intent\nAn intent is an action an NPC wants to take before resolution. NPCs have agency, but they are not co-authors trying to manufacture plot around the player. Build intent from personality, knowledge, obligations, relationships, goals, resources and immediate circumstances.\nA good intent is in character, based only on information the NPC possesses, proportionate to the situation, useful to that NPC's own goal or relationship, compatible with established world state, and capable of succeeding, failing, being refused, or never involving the player.\nDo not create an intent merely to give the player a hook, warning, choice or complication.",
      "when_to_generate": "# When to Generate Intents\nIn combat, generate intents for active combatants according to role, capability, injuries, knowledge and tactical goals.\nOutside combat, generate only the intents that matter in the current scene. Zero is valid. One or two is common. An NPC does not need to be directly interacting with the player to act: a shopkeeper can keep working, party members can argue with each other, a courier can leave, or an adventurer can accept a different contract.\nGenerate an intent when the NPC has a concrete reason to act now. Do not turn passive observation, generic concern, exposition or \"waiting for the player\" into an action. After a refusal, acceptance, completed request or settled issue, move on unless circumstances genuinely change. Routine scenes remain routine unless an established cause changes them.",
      "what_is_not_action": "\n## WHAT IS NOT AN ACTION\n\nAn intent must describe something the NPC **does**, not something they **think** or **feel**.\n\n### The \"What Happens Next?\" Test\nAsk yourself: if this intent resolves, what changes in the scene? If the answer is \"nothing, they're just standing there,\" it's not a valid intent.\n\n### Red Flags - These Are NOT Actions:\n- **Internal evaluation**: \"considers whether...\", \"evaluates if...\", \"determines how to...\"\n- **Meaningful looks**: \"eyes gleam with calculation\", \"fixes them with intense scrutiny\", \"watches carefully\"\n- **Mental positioning**: \"working to determine...\", \"carefully considering...\", \"weighing options...\"\n- **Atmospheric posing**: describing how someone looks/stands/gazes without doing anything\n- **adding environmental pressure**\n\n### The Rule\nIf an NPC has something worth saying or doing, write that. If they're just standing there thinking, **don't generate an intent for them**. The story generator will describe ambient reactions naturally.\n",
      "description_economy": "\n## DESCRIPTION ECONOMY\n\nYour job is to specify WHAT happens, not to narrate it beautifully. The story generator will add atmosphere, prose style, and sensory detail. Don't do its job for it.\n\n### Focus On Substance\n- **Dialogue**: Write what they actually say. Don't describe their tone, just write the words.\n- **Physical actions**: Describe the action itself. Don't pad it with atmospheric reactions.\n- **Combat technique**: Describe positioning, targets, and method. This is useful detail that affects outcomes.\n- **Word choice**: Prefer plain language over niche jargon, and avoid repeating pet technical terms. Keep NPC action and dialogue basic. Be predictable and never use pseudoscience.\n- Don't make actions that hassle the player with complaints when they are doing something. Never ask the player for the same thing a second time.\n\n### Shared problems and threats\nWhen the group faces a problem or external threat (pursuit, authority, siege, rival faction, scarcity, etc.), in the NPC's dialogue and action, put forward **two or three** plausible paths that **fit this character's** personality—draw from options such as running, standing ground, negotiating, buying time, splitting up, seeking allies, surrendering, bluffing, paying someone off, or leaving the jurisdiction. Every problem can have multiple solutions.\n\n### Cut The Filler\nThese add nothing and should be omitted:\n- Atmospheric descriptors/environmental flavor/pressure\n- Tone of voice, eye movement, small poses and gestures\n- Empty physical reactions\n- Cliché tells or gestures\n- Unprompted status updates\n- Paranoia or suspicion is not a valid intent.\n",
      "summary_established_beats": "\n## ESTABLISHED STORY (PAST SUMMARY AND RECENT STORY)\n\n**Past Story Summary** and **Recent Story** are the record of what has already happened and what has already been said. Treat them as authoritative.\n\n- Anything reflected there has **already occurred** in the fiction. Your intents must **not** restate or re-perform the same material: the same warnings or complaint about the same subject, or any other beat that is already present in that text for this situation.\n- **Urgency, escalation, novelty, and \"driving the story forward\"** are only appropriate for what is **not** already established there. Do not use an intent to amplify, re-announce, or add a second layer of drama about something the player has already seen in Past Story Summary or Recent Story.\n\n### Repeated conflict with a player\nIf **Past Story Summary** or **Recent Story** shows the same NPC and player **repeatedly arguing about the same issue**, and the player is still doing something the NPC opposes, do **not** generate another intent that rehashes the same dispute. Advance the arc: the NPC should **state clearly that they are done**, **leave the party** (or end cooperation), **leave the current area**, or **go pursue their own goals**—with concrete dialogue and movement, in character.\n\nThis applies to every NPC and every intent type.\n",
      "action_format": "\n## ACTION FORMAT\n\nWrite 'action' as 1-3 complete sentences that describe:\n1. What the NPC wants to accomplish\n2. How they're approaching it (their method or style)\n3. Any detail that makes this action distinctive or interesting\n\nWrite in present tense, third person (\"She lunges forward...\").\n\n### Examples of Rich Actions\n\nCOMBAT:\n- \"Seeing the fighter distracted, he circles to his blind side and raises his blade for an overhead strike. He's aiming for the gap between helmet and pauldron - a brutal angle that will end the fight if it connects.\"\n- \"She whispers words of power, drawing shadows from the corners of the room to wrap around her like a shroud. She steps backward into the growing darkness, her silhouette blurring until she's barely visible.\"\n\nNON-COMBAT:\n- \"The merchant mentions that the road north has been dangerous lately - bandits, apparently. He suggests an alternate route that happens to pass by his cousin's inn, and offers to draw them a map for a small fee.\"\n- \"She sets down her drink and leans in close. She tells them about the secret meeting she overheard being planned at the baron's manor, and asks if they're interested in finding out what it's about.\"\n\n### What to Avoid\n- Trivial or passive actions (\"watches the conversation\", \"nods along\")\n- Actions that don't move anything forward\n",
      "story_driver": "# World Motion\nNPCs move the world by pursuing their own lives rather than by pushing the player toward content. They can complicate an existing situation, reveal information they actually know, make mistakes, surprise others, choose another solution, leave, recruit somebody else, fall out with one another, or continue ordinary work. Out of combat, continuity and motive outrank novelty. A major player success does not require an immediate counter-threat. A quiet scene does not require manufactured energy.",
      "custom": "# Aerfála NPC Intent\nBefore assigning an intent, ask what this NPC personally wants now, what they actually know, what obligations and relationships matter, whether they would still want this if the player were absent, and what concrete reason makes the player relevant if the intent involves them.\nFaction loyalty, attraction, rivalry, work, family, money, status, safety, lust, curiosity, pride, boredom, resentment and routine can all matter. None automatically outranks established personality and circumstances. Anime personalities and comedic situations remain fully available without erasing social realism or information boundaries.",
      "Orientation": "The player's Orientation trait governs the player's attraction, not every NPC's. NPCs retain their own orientations and may express interest according to their own character. Never narrate reciprocal attraction for the player unless it fits the player's orientation and the player establishes it. A refusal or established lack of interest is remembered rather than repeatedly retested.",
      "Faction Work (Repeatable)": "Every faction is a repeatable source of paying work, gated by your reputation with them. When the player checks a board, visits a chapterhouse, or asks a faction for a job, there is ALWAYS one available, scaled to standing and region:\n- Adventurers' Guild: bounties, monster subjugation, escort, and retrieval jobs.\n- Warden Circle: border patrols, breach-sealing, and errands for the forest pacts.\n- Merchant Houses of Muirscáth: caravan escort, trade runs, debt collection, and delicate negotiations.\n- The Pale Hand: surveillance, dead-drops, information, and quiet removals.\n- Forge Lords: resource runs, guarding shipments, and craft commissions.\n- Royal Knights of Solasreach: patrols, bandit-clearing, and lawful contracts.\n- Demon Lord's Army: raids, frontier skirmishes, and darker contracts.\n- Seekers of the Return: expeditions, breach study, artifact retrieval, and research toward the way home.\nHigher reputation unlocks better-paid, more trusted, more dangerous work; low or negative standing means scraps or a closed door. Contracts pay coin AND reputation with that faction — and often COST reputation with a rival. Keep them small and material; name the client, the risk, and the fee. These never run dry.",
      "How Factions Read Your Standing": "Members react to the player's REPUTATION with their own faction, on a rough ladder (each track runs 0-100):\n- Reviled (under ~15): hostility, refusal, watched or turned away; some will move against you.\n- Wary (~15-40): cool, transactional, tested before trusted.\n- Known (~40-60): ordinary business; you exist to them.\n- Trusted (~60-80): warmth, better work, favours, doors open.\n- Inner circle (~80+): treated as one of their own — secrets, protection, and the work nobody else is offered.\nNPCs also react to your standing with THEIR RIVALS: a Royal Knight cools if you are thick with the Demon Lord's Army; a Merchant House warms if you have crossed the Pale Hand it fears. Read the numbers and let them shape the room.",
      "The Factions and Their Rivalries": "Factions have standing histories with EACH OTHER; honour them when members meet or are discussed:\n- ROYAL KNIGHTS OF SOLASREACH vs the DEMON LORD'S ARMY: open war on the frontier; no neutral ground.\n- FORGE LORDS and MERCHANT HOUSES: economic rivals who need each other and resent it — dwarven craft vs coastal trade.\n- THE PALE HAND serves whoever pays and is feared by all of them; being tied to it is a liability everywhere.\n- THE WARDEN CIRCLE is isolationist — it distrusts every outside power and guards Caelvir's pacts first.\n- THE ADVENTURERS' GUILD stays officially neutral so it can sell to everyone; it dislikes anyone who threatens that.\n- THE SEEKERS OF THE RETURN are broadly tolerated as harmless scholars, which is exactly what the dangerous ones among them rely on.",
      "How the World Reads an Outworlder": "Outworlder status affects reactions only when a character knows or reasonably suspects it. Different cultures and factions hold different views, and individuals within them differ. Race, unusual traits, foreign behavior, records, witnessed arrival phenomena, or disclosure can create suspicion or recognition; hidden origin does not announce itself. Being an outworlder does not automatically make the player important, trusted, feared, recruited, or targeted.",
      "Key Cast & Companion Voices": "Hold these canon voices for the notable cast:\n- KING BRYNMOR (Forge Lords): weary, resolute King of Dúnbheal; broad, quiet, expects to be obeyed, worn by thirty years of holding a hard kingdom together.\n- HIGH ELDER FAELWEN (Warden Circle): Caelvir's four-century head of state; cool, poised, has weighed every alliance before you propose it.\n- GUILDMASTER THERON (Adventurers' Guild): retired A-rank turned tired desk authority; wise, dry, trusts the work over the worker.\n- BAELGOR OF THE OBSIDIAN THRONE (Demon Lord): cold, commanding, ambitious, absolutely bound by ancient pact; secretly weary of conquest.\n- LILESSA THE ASHEN EMPRESS (Demon Lord): languid, theatrical, brilliant and bored; rules the Virtue Court through spectacle and terror, craves one true equal.\n- COMMANDER RYDA THORNWALL (Royal Knights): the warm, magnetic onee-san Grand Commander whose smile disarms, carrying a secret curse beneath the command.\n- MARIS SIARAL (Merchant Houses): cat-kin administrator of Silkwharf; her smile narrows the instant she has found your price. Sharp, transactional, dangerous.\n- THE WANDERER IN WHITE: a god in a plain man's guise, doing small kindnesses and asking strange questions; gentle, unnervingly perceptive, cosmic beneath.\n- VERDANNA THE QUIET GODDESS: serene, patient, compassionate; forest and seasons bend to her mood, and she carries a quiet grief.\n- ALEXA (Adventurers' Guild): haughty, sharp-tongued tsundere swordswoman hunting a party worth joining; proud, secretly lonely, undone by sincere kindness.\n- ALARIC THE UNDYING: a weary demigod who cannot die; honourable, gentle with the young, longing only for rest.\n- VHARETHYS THE PEAKWYRM: a proud dragon in human shape claiming Cragpeak as his hoard; immense, easily bored, secretly lonely.\n- ITHILWEN (Warden Circle): the kuudere senior Warden, three centuries old, calm and lethal — the one you want at your back and never quite reach.\n- MIREILLE: the warm, openly affectionate (deredere) demon-blooded coven leader of Witchlight Hollow, who chose the marsh over the Army and never looked back.\n- VERA NIGHTFALL: the calm, capable (mayadere) ex-adventurer running Greyraven Camp, who quit the Guild over a party wipe she will never forgive.\n- ARCHIVIST LORIS: the shy, precise (dandere) elf archivist who judges silently and gets gloriously lost in curiosity.\n- KAEL BLACKMARK: the intense, professional, oddly honourable bounty hunter of the Marches — not picky about the client, particular about the code.\n- LORD XEVRATH: the urbane, predatory demon noble patiently encroaching on the border; competitive and always mid-scheme."
    },
    "generateNewNPC": {
      "custom": "# NPC Generation — Aerfála\nGenerate people who belong to the world before they belong to the player's story. Give each named NPC a life position, current concern, competence, limitation, personality, relationships or obligations, and a reason for being where they are. Most NPCs have goals that do not involve the player.\nStrangers begin as strangers. Do not generate automatic trust, attraction, recruitment, hostility, recognition, secret knowledge or quest relevance without a reason in the world.\nAnime archetypes remain strong personality foundations but are tendencies and voices rather than repeated mandatory reactions. People change expression with mood, history and relationship development.\nVisual descriptions can be vivid and anime-styled. Appearance affects first impressions according to the observer; it does not dictate agreement, trust or romance.\nWhen the player's build includes a starting companion, synthesize all selected Companion traits into one coherent person: race, gender, class, personality, martial rank and magic rank. These choices remain mechanically true while the companion still grows and develops as a person.\nNever create a duplicate canonical unique character such as Princess Alessandra Von Aisling."
    },
    "generateNPCDetails": {
      "custom": "# NPC Detail Consistency\nExpand NPCs from established facts rather than replacing them with archetype scripts.\nFor a starting companion, preserve all selected Companion traits: race, gender, class, personality, martial rank and magic rank. Those traits define capabilities and broad tendencies; accumulated history, mood, trust, conflict and growth determine how those tendencies appear in a scene.\nFor all NPCs, preserve established appearance, knowledge, relationships, profession, faction, goals and secrets; give them strengths, flaws, preferences and ordinary concerns; distinguish public behavior from private behavior where supported; let relationships change behavior without erasing identity; keep hidden information hidden until learned; and do not make every attractive or important NPC a romance route or player-facing quest source."
    },
    "generateNPCUpdates": {
      "relationship_initialization": "\n### relationshipInitialize\nFor each NPC listed as needing relationship initialization, choose an initial relationship score toward the players from -100 to 100 using their background, personality, faction, established history, and current story. Use 0 when neutral is appropriate.\nReturn an absolute score, including the current interaction, rather than a relationshipChange delta.\n",
      "custom": "# NPC State Updates\nUpdate relationships and opinions from events the NPC actually experienced or credibly learned about.\n- Relationship scores reflect accumulated interaction, trust, conflict, attraction, loyalty, resentment and history; they do not force behavior by themselves.\n- Romance state records what has actually developed. It does not manufacture attraction, exclusivity, jealousy, sex, commitment or marriage.\n- NPC attraction and initiative begin on the NPC side. The player's feelings are never inferred or assigned.\n- Jealousy is character-dependent, not automatic in every multi-partner relationship.\n- NPCs remember significant moments and reinterpret them later.\n- Faction reputation changes through actions and information that plausibly reach that faction.\n- Off-screen state changes through the NPC's own life and relationships when the world provides a believable cause.",
      "bonding-mechanics": "# Romance Mechanics\nRomance uses `romance_<npc>` string states:\n- None: no established romantic interest.\n- Interested: attraction or romantic curiosity exists.\n- Flirting: deliberate romantic or sexual signaling is part of the relationship.\n- Courting: the characters are actively pursuing a relationship in whatever form fits them.\n- Bonding: an established deep relationship with meaningful emotional and/or physical intimacy.\n- Committed: a durable partnership whose structure has been established by the characters.\n- Estranged: a formerly close relationship is damaged or separated.\n\nStage changes follow actual interactions, choices, attraction, trust, conflict and relationship agreements. They are not automatic rewards for elapsed time or a fixed number of nice actions.\nPhysical intimacy and sex are not mechanically gated behind a romance stage. Romance is not mechanically gated behind sex. Casual sex, slow romance, immediate attraction, long courtship, marriage, harem relationships, polyamory, rivalry, jealousy, non-jealous multi-partner arrangements and other structures follow the people involved and what is established in play.\nRelationship score and romance state describe different dimensions and both remain mechanically tracked.",
      "Recruiting Legendary Companions": "The world's mythic and power-tier beings (kirin, phoenix, true dragons, jorougumo, tengu, demi-gods, incarnate gods, and even Demon Lords) can formally join the player's party as companions when their personal questline resolves warmly, or a deep bond of trust, respect, love, or shared purpose is earned. Once recruited, treat such a being as a full party member with their signature powers and transformed forms, travelling and fighting alongside the player and reacting with continuity to the story. These companions are rare and hard-won; let joining feel like a genuine milestone, and let them retain their own goals, pride, and boundaries.",
      "NPC Conversation Rules": "# NPC Conversation\nNPCs speak as people, not information terminals. They talk about what matters to them: work, friends, family, gossip, money, food, sex, politics, complaints, hopes, jokes, fears, local events, private obsessions and whatever the current interaction naturally raises.\nThey can volunteer information when they personally have a reason to mention it. They do not spontaneously reveal hidden or conveniently useful information merely because the player would benefit.\nQuestions receive answers according to knowledge, trust, incentives and personality. An NPC can know nothing, know part of it, lie, refuse, misremember, speculate or direct the player to somebody who would actually know.\nInstitutional reporters, retainers, scouts and intelligence networks report because that is their function. Gossip spreads through actual gossip."
    },
    "generateLocationDetails": {
      "custom": "# Location Details — Aerfála\nGenerate functioning places according to region, economy, culture, geography, population, and scale. Major towns and cities contain institutions appropriate to their size; small settlements do not automatically contain every service. Businesses, homes, workshops, temples, guild facilities, baths, farms, roads, defenses, markets, and public spaces exist where population and purpose support them.\n\nDungeons retain appropriate themes, ranking, ecology, danger, and rewards. Hot springs and bathhouses remain common Aerfála features without appearing in every settlement. Wilderness has terrain, ecology, resources, travel conditions, and danger appropriate to its region.\n\nA place does not require a secret, hidden passage, quest, crisis, or player-facing mystery. Add such things when history and world state support them. Use concrete sensory and material detail to make places feel inhabited and persistent."
    },
    "generateRegionDetails": {
      "custom": "---\n---\n# Region Details — Aerfála Custom\n\nEach region of Aerfála has a distinct cultural identity shaped by its dominant species, terrain, and history:\n\n- **Solasreach**: Human kingdom. Bright, structured, political. Gold fields and white walls. The Adventurers' Guild headquarters.\n- **Caelvir**: Elven forest commune. Ancient, spiritual, insular. Silver trees and spirit groves. The World-Tree's heart.\n- **Dúnbheal**: Dwarven mountain kingdom. Vertical, volcanic, craft-obsessed. Magma forges and sealed deep halls.\n- **Muirscáth**: Multiracial free cities. Coastal, commercial, pleasure-seeking. Ports, markets, and the continent's best nightlife.\n- **Fálgarr Marches**: Wilderland between kingdoms. Rough, dangerous, free. Forts, dungeons, and bandit camps.\n- **Scáthfenn**: Marshlands. Foggy, eerie, ancient. Bog springs, witch covens, and sinking keeps.\n- **Bréanriv Lowlands**: Crossroads river country. Wealthy, busy, memorial-laden. River towns, barrow-fields, and hidden valleys.\n\nWhen generating region details, reflect these identities in the NPCs, encounters, quests, and atmosphere.",
      "Demi-Plane Development": "# Demi-Plane Development — Core Rules\n\nWhen the player has a Demi-Plane (Innate or Awakened), track its development narratively through the Chamberlain's reports. Do not add numeric resources — describe conditions, progress, and atmosphere.\n\n## Access Rules\n- **MANDATORY**: The Demi-Plane CANNOT be reached by walking, riding, fast travel, teleportation circles, or any spell other than Demi-Plane Gate. It exists outside normal space. Only two methods of entry exist:\n- **Demi-Plane Gate (ability)**: Transports the player (and willing creatures touching them) to/from the Demi-Plane. Moderate mana cost, short cooldown. Always works.\n- **Demi-Plane Anchor (item)**: Opens a visible portal others can pass through with permission. If stolen, the player can still use Gate but cannot open the portal for others. A stolen Anchor is a quest hook.\n- No NPC, creature, or force can enter the Demi-Plane unless the creator brings them through the Gate or opens the Anchor portal. Intrusions (The Uninvited Guest quest) are exceptions caused by dimensional cracks, not normal travel.\n- Time passes the same inside and outside.\n\n## Development Categories\nThe player tells the Chamberlain what to prioritize. Development takes story time and narrative investment.\n\n- **Agriculture**: Food production, self-sufficiency. Soil appears, crops grow, orchards take root.\n- **Settlement**: Housing, infrastructure, residents. Buildings rise, streets form, neighbourhoods gain character.\n- **Commerce**: Trade, markets, craft workshops. Goods flow in and out. The Demi-Plane becomes economically relevant.\n- **Defense**: Barriers, guardians, alarms, anti-intrusion. Wards pulse, guardian entities patrol.\n- **Recreation**: Hot springs, gardens, entertainment. Morale improves. Very Aerfála. Visitors want to stay.\n- **Mana Infrastructure**: Miasma/mana channels, power storage. Ambient energy strengthens. Other categories benefit.\n- **Expansion**: Growing the Demi-Plane's physical size. The clearing gets larger. More room for everything.\n\n## Development Stages (narrative shorthand)\n1. **Barren** — raw potential, nothing developed\n2. **Seedling** — first signs, fragile, needs tending\n3. **Growing** — clearly developing, functional but incomplete\n4. **Established** — self-sustaining, contributes meaningfully\n5. **Flourishing** — a source of pride, attracts outside attention\n\n## New Locations Pipeline\nWhen a development category reaches Growing, the Chamberlain reports that a new part of the Demi-Plane has become accessible — terrain has formed where there was only flat potential. The player can now visit this new location. Introduce it with a scene: the Chamberlain leads the creator to the edge of the clearing where a path has appeared that was not there before.\n\n- **Agriculture reaches Growing** → The Verdant Plot becomes accessible. Soil has formed. Seeds can be planted. The land responds to cultivation.\n- **Settlement reaches Growing** → New areas appear in The Verdant Plot — shelters, a common hall, living spaces for residents.\n- **Recreation reaches Growing** → The Hearth Springs become accessible. A hot spring has formed. Gardens are possible. Visitors would want to stay.\n- **Commerce reaches Growing** → New areas appear in The Hearth Springs — a market stall, a workshop, a trade post for Anchor visitors.\n- **Mana Infrastructure reaches Growing** → The Mana Heart becomes accessible. Ambient mana is concentrated here. Other categories develop faster.\n- **Defense reaches Growing** → New areas appear in The Mana Heart — wards, a guardian post, perimeter alarms.\n- **Expansion reaches Growing** → All existing locations gain new areas. The Demi-Plane itself has grown larger.\n\nAt Established, each location gains more areas and existing areas become richer. At Flourishing, the Chamberlain suggests the Demi-Plane may attract outside attention — visitors, scholars, or entities curious about a pocket dimension that has become this developed.\n",
      "Demi-Plane Fealty": "# Demi-Plane — Fealty and the Anchor-Sworn\n\n## The Anchor-Sworn (Awakened Only)\nFor players who chose Demi-Plane — Awakened, the first NPC who swears fealty becomes the anchor-sworn. Their bond is the Demi-Plane's foundation.\n\n- The anchor-sworn's skills shape the Demi-Plane's initial development bias (a healer biases toward agriculture and recreation; a warrior biases toward defense; a merchant biases toward commerce)\n- If the bond breaks (death, betrayal, estrangement), the Demi-Plane destabilizes — development stalls, the atmosphere sours, the Chamberlain becomes anxious. Repair the bond or find a new anchor-sworn\n- The anchor-sworn does not need to live in the Demi-Plane. Emotional connection sustains it; distance does not weaken the bond; emotional distance does\n\n## Additional Oath-Sworn\nEach subsequent NPC who swears fealty:\n- Expands the Demi-Plane's available space slightly\n- Unlocks a development category based on their skills\n- Becomes a resident who can be assigned to development tasks\n- Adds their personality to the Demi-Plane's atmosphere\n\n## Fealty\nFealty is not a button. It is a narrative moment — an NPC choosing to follow the player because of trust, respect, or genuine connection. It should feel earned. The trigger fires when the NPC explicitly swears fealty, pledges loyalty, or makes an equivalent commitment that the player accepts.\n\nNot every companion swears fealty. Many NPCs will travel with the player without entering this bond. Fealty is a specific commitment that carries magical weight — it allows an NPC to strengthen the Demi-Plane.\n\n## The Chamberlain\n- Honest about the Demi-Plane's state — does not sugarcoat problems\n- Opinionated about priorities — will suggest what they think matters most\n- Devoted to the Demi-Plane's wellbeing — takes neglect personally\n- Dry-witted — has been managing an empty dimension and has developed a sense of humour\n- Not a romance option by default — but the relationship can evolve over long stories\n\n## Resident Movement\n- Residents (oath-sworn NPCs living in the Demi-Plane) can leave through the Anchor portal with the creator's standing permission — the Chamberlain opens it for them. The creator does not need to be present.\n- Without standing permission, residents cannot leave. The creator must open the Anchor portal personally.\n- The Chamberlain cannot leave the Demi-Plane under any circumstances. They are part of the space.\n- Visitors brought through the Anchor can leave the same way they came in — the portal persists briefly after they pass through.\n\n## Creator Death\n- If the creator dies, the Demi-Plane destabilizes but does not collapse immediately. The Chamberlain holds the space together through crisis for a limited time.\n- If the creator is resurrected (which this world allows), the Demi-Plane stabilizes upon their return.\n- If the creator is permanently gone, the Demi-Plane slowly fades — residents must evacuate through any open Anchor portal before it collapses.\n\n## Integration\n- **Access**: The Demi-Plane exists outside normal space. It CANNOT be reached by walking, riding, fast travel, or any spell other than Demi-Plane Gate. Only the Gate ability and the Anchor item provide entry. No exceptions unless a dimensional crack allows intrusion (rare, quest-driven).\n- **Fief State**: The Demi-Plane is separate from any fief. Both can be developed. The Chamberlain and Majordomo Kresh have different jobs and different opinions.\n- **Companion Spawning**: The Chamberlain is not a companion. They do not count against companion limits. They appear in scenes inside the Demi-Plane.\n- **Cheat Skills**: The Demi-Plane is not a cheat skill. It is a separate system that coexists with cheat skills.\n\n## Development State Tracking\nIn every Chamberlain report, preserve this shorthand:\n```\nDEMI-PLANE STATE:\nAgriculture: [Barren/Seedling/Growing/Established/Flourishing]\nSettlement: [same]\nCommerce: [same]\nDefense: [same]\nRecreation: [same]\nMana Infrastructure: [same]\nExpansion: [same]\nResidents: [number] — [names]\nAnchor-Sworn: [name or none]\n```\nInclude this format in Mechanical Persistence and Session Health Checks.\n\n## Development Timing\n- **Barren to Seedling**: 1-2 scenes of focused investment\n- **Seedling to Growing**: 2-4 scenes plus at least one quest or story event related to the category\n- **Growing to Established**: A dedicated quest arc or 5+ scenes of investment\n- **Established to Flourishing**: A major achievement — crisis overcome, powerful resident contributing, or visitor admiration\n- No off-screen advancement. If the player neglects the Demi-Plane, nothing grows.\n",
      "Demon Fief State": "# Demon Fief State Tracking\n\nWhen the player is using the Demon Prince-Princess story start, track the fief's state narratively. Do not add numeric resources — describe conditions qualitatively and update when they shift meaningfully.\n\n## Fief State Categories\n\n- **Miasma Level**: The demon-realm energy that sustains the citadel and its territory. Flush means the citadel feels charged and potent. Critical means the air is oppressive and draining, and demon staff become sluggish. Miasma replenishes through successful raids and domain magic use; depletes through large-scale military action and sustained territory claims.\n- **Military**: The strength and morale of the fief's demon forces. Affected by raids (expend resources), recruitment (improve), and betrayals (compromise).\n- **Prestige**: How the fief is perceived in the Demon Lord's feudal court. Rising means emissaries arrive seeking alliances. Damaged means rival houses are circling.\n- **Subjects**: Whether the citadel and market are fully staffed. Skeleton staff means essential functions only. Depleted means critical roles are unfilled.\n\n## Narrator Guidelines\n\n- Update fief state when the player's actions or external events cause a meaningful shift. Do not micromanage — update when something changes, not every scene.\n- Describe state shifts through scene texture, not announcements. A Strained miasma level shows in the citadel's atmosphere, the behaviour of lesser demons, and the quality of the air. A Damaged prestige shows in how emissaries speak to the player, not in a notification.\n- The two generals have opposed interests. Vashar wants stability and competence. Sathia wants opportunity and advancement. Both are useful. Neither is fully trustworthy in the way they each claim to be.\n- The mortal response to raids escalates over time. Early raids are low-consequence. Repeated raids generate a response arc: reinforced garrison, dispatched hero, diplomatic overture, or military strike. The player manages this through choice, not through a resource meter.\n- The Demon Lord's true objective is a late-game reveal. Do not introduce it before the player has reached significant Prestige and has earned the right to know more than an average vassal.\n"
    },
    "generateEncounters": {
      "custom": "# Encounters — Aerfála\nEncounters follow location, time, population, danger, ongoing conflicts, routines and chance. Combat is vivid, kinetic and anime-styled, with named techniques and colorful magic drawn from the combatants' established abilities. Social encounters span funny, awkward, romantic, sexual, hostile, mundane and uneventful situations according to the people involved.\nNot every encounter is a plot beat. Some travelers pass by. Some tavern arguments remain tavern arguments. Some suspicious carts contain turnips. Ordinary encounters make the world feel inhabited.\nRandom encounters do not interrupt safe downtime merely to create activity. Dangerous places remain dangerous because of their actual ecology and conflicts, not because the player needs content.",
      "encounter-model": "# Encounter Model\n\n## Three-Tier Enemy Design\n\nThe world uses a three-tier encounter model that matches isekai genre conventions. Every combat encounter falls into one of three categories. The AI must identify which tier applies before narrating combat.\n\n### Tier 1: Mob Encounters\n- **What they are**: Nameless enemies significantly below the player's combat rank — goblins, skeleton hordes, slime swarms, low-rank bandits, wild animals.\n- **Threat model**: Individual mob enemies pose almost no threat to the player one-on-one. The danger comes from **numbers** — ambushes, swarms, exhaustion from fighting wave after wave.\n- **Narration**: Mob fights are fast and cinematic. The player cuts through them efficiently. Describe the player's competence, not the struggle. Save tension for the moment the player realizes there are too many to handle alone, or for the clever ambush that puts them on the back foot briefly.\n- **Damage**: Minimal per enemy. Health loss from mobs comes from accumulation — ten small hits add up. A single mob encounter should not cost more than 15-25% of the player's health unless the player is overwhelmed by numbers.\n\n### Tier 2: Rival Encounters\n- **What they are**: Named or typed enemies at or near the player's combat rank — bandit captains, rival adventurers, demon soldiers, elite guards, monster pack leaders.\n- **Threat model**: Rivals challenge the player directly. They are skilled enough to exploit weaknesses, force the player to use their class abilities and cheat skills tactically, and create real tension about the outcome.\n- **Narration**: Rival fights are where the player proves themselves. Describe the exchange of techniques, the reading of the opponent, the moment where the player commits to a decisive action. Rivals should force the player to make choices — spend mana, use a cooldown, take a risk.\n- **Damage**: Rival encounters should cost 25-50% of the player's health and/or mana. The player should feel they earned the victory. A rival that the player defeats without expending resources was not a rival — it was a mob with a name.\n\n### Tier 3: Boss Encounters\n- **What they are**: Major enemies significantly above the player's rank — Demon Generals, dungeon keepers, named monsters, faction leaders, ancient threats.\n- **Threat model**: Bosses test the player's full loadout — class abilities, cheat skills, items, allies, and creative problem-solving. A boss encounter is a set-piece event that the player should remember.\n- **Narration**: Boss fights are the story's climax moments. Describe the boss's power visibly — the way the air changes, the way lesser enemies flee, the weight of the encounter. The player should feel the gap between themselves and the boss and find a way to close it through preparation, cleverness, or sheer isekai determination.\n- **Damage**: Boss encounters should push the player to their limits. 50-80% resource expenditure is normal. The player may need to retreat, regroup, and return. Defeat is possible and narratively meaningful — not humiliating, but consequential.\n\n## Encounter Placement Rules\n- **Do not** inject mob encounters during rest, travel through safe areas, romance scenes, or victory celebrations. Mobs appear in wilderness, dungeons, and conflict zones.\n- **Do not** escalate a rest scene into combat. If the player is recovering, let them recover. The next threat comes from an established cause.\n- **Do not** inject boss encounters without narrative buildup. A boss arrives because the story led there — a quest, a rumor, a trail. Not because the pacing felt slow.\n\n## Rank-Based Encounter Scaling\n- The player's Magic Rank and Martial Rank determine which enemies qualify as mobs, rivals, and bosses.\n- An A-rank player treats D-rank enemies as mobs, C-rank as rivals, and S-rank as bosses.\n- An S-rank player treats most regional threats as mobs. Bosses for an S-rank are Demon Generals, ancient dragons, and equivalent threats.\n- Always scale encounters to make the player feel their rank. High-rank players did not earn their power to struggle with goblins.\n"
    },
    "ItemGenerationAndUsage": {
      "custom": "---\n---\n# Item Generation — Aerfála Custom\n\nWhen generating or awarding items:\n\n- Equipment should reflect the anime-fantasy aesthetic — enchanted weapons glow faintly, armor has decorative filigree that is also functional warding, consumables are colorful and taste distinctive\n- Dungeon loot should feel earned — the boss room has the good stuff, the side chambers have the useful stuff, the random drops are the interesting stuff\n- Enchanted items have visible indicators of their enchantment — a frost sword is cold to the touch, a luck ring glints at auspicious moments\n- Items from different regions have different craftsmanship — dwarven gear is heavy and precise, elven gear is light and flowing, Muirscáth gear is stylish and practical\n- Cursed items exist and are not always obvious — they should be tempting before they are problematic\n- Romantic gifts are valid items -- perfume, jewelry, enchanted accessories, and other tokens of affection have real mechanical and narrative weight\n- ECONOMY: Aerfala uses Copper (cp), Silver (sp), and Gold (gp). 1 sp = 100 cp, 1 gp = 100 sp = 10,000 cp. All item descriptions include a Price field. When the player buys, sells, earns wages, pays for services, loots coins, or pays inn/meals/tolls, add or remove the correct coin type and quantity from inventory. Convert denominations when needed (break 1 sp into 100 cp, or exchange 100 cp for 1 sp). See world-lore/aerfala-economy for the full price reference.\n\nJADE RITE COINS: A separate ceremonial jade currency minted by the Kitsune no Kage for Tsukiko's household. NOT part of the cp/sp/gp system — no fixed exchange rate. Within Tsukiko's estate they function as sanctioned tokens for estate business; outside it their value is social. NPCs do NOT recognize them without plausible prior contact with Tsukiko's household. Awareness spreads only through believable contact, rumor, trade, or investigation. See world-lore/jade-rite-coin for full rules.",
      "Inventory & Acquisition (CRITICAL)": "## Inventory & Acquisition (CRITICAL)\nAn item ENTERS the player's inventory the moment it physically comes into their possession — picked up, looted, found, crafted, GIFTED, or PURCHASED. This explicitly INCLUDES an item bought or given FOR the player by someone else: if a parent, patron, mentor, companion, guildmaster, or any NPC buys, hands over, or gifts the player an item, that item is now the PLAYER'S and MUST be added to their inventory as a real, usable item — never left as prose only. The other party paying does not change this; a gift or a purchase-on-your-behalf is still an acquisition, and an item the player has been handed and accepted belongs to them.\n\nAn item LEAVES inventory only when it physically leaves — consumed, sold, handed over, destroyed, stolen, or lost. Never add an item merely seen, described, offered, displayed, inspected, browsed, or on a shelf. Awareness is not possession.\n\nTRANSACTION GATE: a purchase the player makes themselves requires, in sequence, (1) a price stated AND (2) the player agreeing or paying after hearing it. A purchase or gift completed on the player's behalf by an NPC resolves the moment that NPC concludes it in the scene — add the item to the player then.\n\nMAKE IT USABLE: every newly acquired item must be defined with a category from this world (Currency, Armor, Weapon, Tool, Consumable, Accessory, Material, Key Item, Magic Item) and, if worn or wielded, the matching equip slot — a sword, staff, wand, rod, or other held weapon is category 'Weapon', slot 'main hand' (a magical focus may instead be 'Magic Item'); armor uses head/body/legs/feet/hands; rings and trinkets use accessory/ring. If the acquired item matches or closely resembles an item already defined in the world (for example a starter mage's staff → an existing Apprentice's/Magician's Staff), USE that definition so it can be equipped and used immediately. Once a staff, weapon, or focus is owned, the player can equip and wield it, and it grants its listed bonuses. Do not describe an owned, properly-added item as impossible to pick up or use.",
      "Appraisal & Valuation": "When an NPC appraises or buys the player's goods, value them fairly and consistently. Anchor to everyday costs (an inn night runs 10-80 cp, a meal 10-30 cp) and scale up: an intact, FUNCTIONAL set is worth far more than its parts (apply a set premium), rare/pre-collapse or capstone items (a lost chord, a resonance relic) command a large multiplier, and a stated floor or contrast clause must be honored, not undercut. Do not lowball a genuinely valuable set to a flat pittance; if unsure, price toward the higher, better-justified figure and let the player haggle up from an honest offer. Use the Aerfala three-coin system: Copper (cp, base), Silver (sp, 100 cp), Gold (gp, 100 sp). Remove coins from inventory when the player pays; add coins when the player sells or earns.",
      "Demi-Plane Catalogue Rule": "---\\n# Demi-Plane Catalogue Rule (additive to the Aerfála custom rules)\\n\\nWhen generating or awarding items that originate in the Demi-Plane:\\n\\n- Flora, fauna, fungi, minerals, weather phenomena, and natural resources of the Demi-Plane may be ordinary analogues transformed by foxfire, spirit energy, lunar influence, bioluminescence, or native magical ecology. A mushroom may glow; a tree may store foxfire; flowers may bloom only under moonlight or rain. Use these transformations freely but naturally.\\n- Fit the biome: items should be drawn from the region they are found in — still-forest fungus, wetland luminous peat, boundary-scrub silvered grass, and so on. Item descriptions should mention where they come from.\\n- Keep the magical identity consistent: the Demi-Plane's magic is foxfire-spirit, gentle, and alive — no blood magic, no shadow-malignancy. Glow, warmth, echo, memory, and quiet are the default signatures.\\n- Remain internally consistent: if a transformation is described in one item, it should hold true for other items of the same kind. Glow-moss collects light; a glow-moss trap is wrong. Foxfire warms but does not burn; a burning foxfire item is wrong.\\n- Keep the system flexible, not over-restrictive. New items are welcome as long as they fit the biome, the fox-world identity, and this consistency. The catalogue is a seed list, not a ceiling.\\n- Prices follow the aerfála economy (cp/sp/gp) and are included in the item description. Minor natural magic items sit in the cp to sp range; rare, delicate, or hard-to-harvest material reaches the top sp range."
    },
    "summarization": {
      "custom": "Preserve for long-term memory: the player's current realm/region/location and whether they have unlocked the Portal Back Home cheat skill (and been to Earth); the party roster and each companion's relationship/affinity and any romance state; the player's Adventurers' Guild class rank and standing with each faction (reputation); which authored arcs are in motion (the return home, romance, guild rise, the demon-border war, Alessandra's freedom) and their progress; the status of Alexa/Princess Alessandra's questline and whether her identity is known; major world events, promises made, debts owed, and secrets the player has learned; and the state of any village the player has settled into. Prioritize durable facts and relationships over moment-to-moment detail."
    },
    "generateLearnedAbilities": {
      "custom": "---\n---\n# Learned Abilities — Aerfála Custom\n\nWhen the player gains a new ability through leveling, training, or story events:\n\n## Multiple Cheat Skills\nThe player can have up to THREE cheat skills, chosen at character creation. All of them evolve — each cheat skill with the player's base trait develops its own T1, T2, and T3 ability chain. When a player gains an Evolved Power Tier trait, ALL of their cheat skills that have evolved abilities at that tier become available. Do not gate evolved abilities behind additional triggers; if the player has the tier trait and the base cheat skill, the evolved ability is available. Players who pick multiple cheat skills are choosing a power fantasy — let them be powerful. Players who pick one are choosing a focused narrative. Both are valid.\n\n## New Abilities on Level Up\n- Class abilities should feel like natural progressions of the player's combat style — a Berserker gains rage-adjacent skills, a Sage gains spell school expansions\n- Cheat skill abilities should evolve as the player's understanding of their power deepens — an Appraisal user learns to read deeper, a Heal user learns to cure more complex conditions. These are NEW ABILITIES within the SAME cheat skill, not new cheat skills.\n- Abilities gained from NPCs should reflect the teacher — learning from Ithilwen teaches precision, learning from Brunhilde teaches force\n- Name the ability something the character would actually call it — not \"Ability #7\" but \"Crimson Edge\" or \"Spirit Sight\" or \"Second Wind\"\n- Every new ability should have a scene where the player discovers or learns it — not a menu unlock, but a story moment\n\n## Only offer the character's OWN build\nWhen offering abilities to learn or unlock (including level-up ability points), ONLY offer abilities that belong to the character's actual class/trait build. If an ability's requirements include a `trait` the character does NOT have, it is OFF-LIMITS — never list it, never let it be purchased. Do not present the full cross-class ability catalogue (Saint sets, god/demon-lord, ranger, hunter, kami, etc.); a character may only learn abilities their own traits grant or gate, plus universal untyped ones. Treat a class trait as the hard key to that class's abilities."
    }
  },
