      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> The wrongness of the sky prickles at the back of your neck like a half-remembered dream. You sit up slowly, palms flat against the unnervingly smooth ground, and the pale stone arch behind you hums a low, patient note — a heartbeat that is not yours. The air is clean, still, and faintly warm, like the inside of a shrine before dawn.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> The Chamberlain watches you rise with the mild, unhurried interest of someone who has seen a thousand awakenings and found none of them surprising. Their clipboard taps once against their palm. Their smile is precise, professional, and just shy of amused.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [calmly] You are taking the news better than most. Some people scream. One person tried to fight the sky. I do not recommend it. The sky does not fight back, which is somehow worse.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You feel the weight of the Demi-Plane Anchor in your pocket — a small, warm stone that hums in time with the arch. Your fox ears flick, catching the faint, hollow acoustics of a space that has never held a sound before. The clearing stretches endlessly in every direction, flat and featureless, a canvas with no paint.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [dryly] The ground responds to intention, you know. Walk to a spot, imagine what belongs there, and the earth will remember. It is a slow process. Like raising a child, if the child were a landscape and the landscape had no opinions of its own.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> They gesture with the clipboard toward the arch. The pale stone shimmers faintly, a doorway that leads somewhere you have not yet decided to go.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [matter-of-fact] The door behind you works both ways. Step through and you are back where you were. Step back through and you are here. The Anchor lets you open the door for others — once there is anything worth showing them.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> The clearing waits. The 