ver gone.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> The Chamberlain has stopped walking. They stand at the far corner of the perimeter wall, one heel still pressed into the soil where they were marking the next foundation point — a point that no longer needs marking. The wall is already there, solid and warm, rising above them. Their ledger hangs open in one hand. Their pen is in the other. Neither has moved in some time. They look slowly from the wall to the gate to the pavilion to the lake to the foxfire lanterns drifting above the bridges, and their mouth opens, closes, opens again.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [flatly] I had a sequence. Gatehouse, main hall, wings, gardens. A sensible order. I was recording each section as it solidified.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [quietly] I got the gatehouse.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> They look down at the ledger. One entry. Beside it, a long blank column where the rest of the sequence was supposed to go. They close the ledger, tuck it under their arm, and press both hands flat against the perimeter wall as if checking whether it is real. It is. Their fingers curl slightly against the warm clay.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [evenly] I will not say it again. But I have revised my understanding of you a second time, and I would appreciate it if you did not require a third.",
