      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] I came from a place very far from here — past forests older than the roads that cross them. I stopped because the earth was crying. I could hear it. That is... not something I can walk past.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You set the bowl on the bench beside you and turn to face Brynja fully. Your hands rest inside your sleeves. The morning light catches the faint luminescent trace along your collarbone — a thread of soft pink glow beneath the silk, there and gone as you shift.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] I will not claim a title I have not earned in your eyes. But I can tell you this truthfully: the soil will recover. What I cleansed was the hex, not the land itself. The land remembers what it was before the poison. Give it water and time, and it will remember faster than you expect.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's breath catches — a small, sharp thing she covers by pressing her knuckles against her apron. Her ears tremble once, then steady. She holds your gaze for a long moment, and something behind her eyes shifts: the careful mistrust of a woman who has been disappointed by promises doesn't vanish, but it steps aside to make room for something else.\n\nBrynja: [steadily] How long will you stay? If you're staying, the north path needs someone who can hold it. I'd rather know now than hope later.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] I cannot say how long I will remain. That is not evasion — it is honesty. I go where I am needed, and I do not always know where that will be until I arrive. But while I am here, the north path is not something you need to face alone.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hazel> Hazel's ears snap upright. She bounces on her toes, tail whipping behind her, and leans past Brynja's arm.\n\nHazel: [breathless] I saw the broken staff! By the wall! It was all black and cracked and — can you show me the light? The purification one? Just a little, just once? I want to tell the others I saw real magic!\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You look at the girl. Her amber eyes are fierce and bright, her small fists clenched at her sides with the desperate hope of a child who wants to believe the world has something in it that can fix things. Your expression softens — not a smile, but the quiet warmth that precedes one.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] I can show you a little. But I want you to remember something more important than the light itself.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You lift one hand from your sleeve, palm up. A small sphere of pale light blooms above your fingers — soft, warm, the color of moonlight through cherry petals. It casts no shadow. It makes no sound. It simply is, gentle and steady, and the air around it smells faintly of clean rain.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hazel> Hazel goes absolutely still. Her ears flatten, her tail freezes, her mouth drops open. The light reflects in her wide amber eyes like a tiny moon caught in glass.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] The light is not the point. The point is that the dirt deserved someone to notice it was hurting. Remember that, and you will already be further along than most who carry magic.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hazel> Hazel nods once, hard, jaw set with a seriousness far beyond her years. Then the grin breaks through — enormous, gap-toothed, incandescent — and she spins toward the lane.\n\nHazel: [shouting] SORREL! CAMPION! COME SEE! COME SEE THE LIGHT!",
