      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You follow Brynja up the lane, your naginata resting across your back. The residential row thins as you walk — fewer garden gates, longer gaps between cottages, the hedgerows growing wilder and less trimmed. The packed earth of the lane gives way to a rougher track bordered by low stone walls gone green with moss.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja stops at a plot where the wall dips to knee height. Beyond it, a garden stretch runs thirty paces along the north boundary — rows of bean poles, a few late squash vines, and three bare patches of soil that look like something burned them from underneath. She drives the hoe into the earth and waits.\n\nBrynja: [flatly] This is mine. The black spots are where the hex surfaced. But the roots — [she kneels and pulls a bean vine free, showing the white threads blackened halfway down] — they carry it further. The surface heals. The underground doesn't. Not on its own.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You kneel beside her. The soil beneath the surface layer is cool and damp but carries a faint sourness — not the sharp rot of active hex, but the dull residue of something that passed through and left its taste behind. The root threads Brynja holds are blackened but not dead. They cling to the vine with the stubbornness of something still trying to grow.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] The hex is gone. What remains is the wound it left. The roots are alive — see how they still grip the vine? Dead roots let go. These are holding on.\n\nBrynja: [quietly] The Ashfords' plot is next. Fifty paces east. [she pulls the hoe free and glances at you] Can you feel it? The sourness? Or do you need to dig?\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] I can feel it. It is faint, but it is there — like a bruise beneath healed skin. The land will push it out on its own, but it will take weeks. Water will speed it. So will turning the soil where the roots run deepest.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja stands, brushes earth from her knees, and looks at you for a long moment. Her tail sways once — a single slow sweep — and something in her shoulders loosens by a fraction.\n\nBrynja: [gruffly] Good. Then you see what I see. Come on — the Ashfords' plot's worse than mine.",
