      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja shoulders the hoe and walks the low wall east, her boots finding the track without hesitation. Fifty paces along, the wall rises again to chest height where it meets a sturdier section reinforced with flat river stones. Beyond it, the Ashfords' plot stretches wider than Brynja's — neat rows of early spring greens gone patchy, three broad stretches of soil darkened to charcoal where the hex broke through.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> She stops at the midpoint and drives the hoe in deep, levering up a wedge of dark earth. The soil crumbles apart, revealing root threads that run black twice as far as the ones in her own garden — some tracing all the way to the neighboring bean rows.\n\nBrynja: [flatly] Here's where it's worst. The Ashfords lost their whole winter squash bed. Roots ran under the wall into my plot from this side — that's how it spread. [she crouches and scrapes a furrow with her fingers] Feel this. The sourness is thicker here. Two hands down it's still warm, and not from the sun.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You press your palm flat against the turned earth. The residue is heavier here — a sluggish, taint-like sourness that clings to your fingertips even after you lift your hand. The roots beneath are alive but discolored, threading sideways through the soil in dark veins that follow the water lines the Ashfords dug for irrigation.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] Turn the soil here — don't wait. The roots are carrying it along the irrigation channels. If you let it sit, it will reach the well plot before the week is out. Turn it deep, break the root threads, and flood the furrows. The water will dilute what the roots held.\n\nBrynja: [nodding] I'll tell Ashford tonight. He's stubborn, but he'll listen when the roots are this dark. [she stands, wiping her hands on her apron] The well plot's last. Come on.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hazel> Two hundred paces south, Hazel has crept along the hedge to the