      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You pass Hazel at the hedge line. Your hand finds the top of her head before she can flinch — fingers settling between her grey-tabby ears, pressing gently, thumb stroking the soft fur at her temple. She goes rigid for half a heartbeat, then leans into it.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] You have sharp eyes, Hazel. Sharper than most. That is a gift — and a duty. [a pause] But a sentry who collapses from exhaustion sees nothing. Know your limit. Rest before you reach it, not after. The wall will still be here tomorrow.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hazel> Hazel's ears fold flat under your palm. Her jaw tightens. She does not argue — but her tail curls around her own ankle, and her amber eyes stay fixed on the treeline even as she nods once, small and stiff, against your hand.\n\nHazel: [quietly] I know my limit.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You leave her there and catch up to Brynja, who has already reached the far end of the north wall where the old well rises from a tangle of moss and clover. The common garden spreads behind it — shared plots marked by low stone borders, the soil darker and richer here than in the private gardens.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja crouches at the garden's north edge and scrapes a furrow with two fingers. The earth parts easily, damp and dark, but the root threads beneath run crooked and discolored — black veins branching deeper than in either of the other plots. She rubs the soil between her fingers and frowns.\n\nBrynja: [calling back] Come feel this. The roots run twice as deep here — the well water feeds them. If it spreads into the well itself, we lose the whole garden.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Hazel> Hazel's ears snap flat against her skull. Her whole body goes rigid. Her gaze locks on the treeline where the north path vanishes into shadow, and her hand closes around the grass braid until her knuckles go white.\n\nHazel: [whispering] Something moved. At the trees. [a beat, barely audible] It's not a deer.",
