      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You straighten from the furrow and turn your head toward the rain barrel behind Brynja's shed — and in the same motion, your gaze sweeps the treeline. The shadow is closer now. Halfway between the third trunk and the hedge's end, belly-flat, moving in increments too deliberate to be an animal foraging. Your ears catch the faint scrape of claws on dry leaf litter, then silence.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly] Brynja. The barrel — go. I'll watch the trench.\n\nBrynja: [low] Something's there.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] I know. Go get the water. Walk normally. I have this.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's grey eyes hold yours for a beat — measuring, deciding. Then she wipes her blackened hands on her apron, stands, and walks south toward the shed at the same steady pace she does everything else. Her tail stays bristled. Her ears don't stop tracking backward.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature freezes the instant Brynja moves. Its pale eyes flick between the retreating figure and the one still crouched at the trench — then settle on you. It doesn't advance. It holds, belly pressed to the earth, reading you the way a fox reads a trap: circling the question of whether the still shape by the well is prey or something else entirely.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You keep your hands in the trench, fingers working the broken root threads aside, clearing the channel for flooding. Your posture stays bent, unhurried, your breathing even. But your weight shifts subtly onto your back foot, and the hand resting near the hoe's handle goes still — ready, not reaching. The morning light catches the faintest glow along the nape of your neck where the bioluminescent markings sleep beneath your collar, stirring without your permission.",
