      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> The foxfire on your fingertip dims — not snuffed, but gently folded back into your skin, the pale gold light sinking beneath the surface like a stone into still water. The bioluminescent markings along your nake soften from their full bloom to a drowsy shimmer, cherry-blossom branches settling back into dormant ink. You lower yourself into a crouch at the edge of the flooded furrow, silk robes pooling in the mud without concern, and reach across the water to rest your palm on the creature's wedge-shaped head.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] Good. Now you'll answer me. And don't you worry — I'll understand your tongue.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature flinches at the touch — then goes rigidly still beneath your hand. Its fur is cold and coarse, damp from the flooded furrow, and the muscles beneath twitch once before locking flat. Its pale eyes slide sideways up to your face, reading the absence of flame, the crouch, the hand on its skull. A long, shuddering breath pushes through its throat. Then the rattling hiss returns — but it shifts, dropping in pitch, lengthening between pauses, the guttural cadence resolving into something patterned. Not animal sound. Language. Broken, halting, pressed through a throat never meant for speech, but language — a dialect of the Old Tongue that hasn't been spoken in the Marches for centuries, its vocabulary stripped down to the bare bones of threat, hunger, and territory.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature's hissing speech comes in fragments. *Roots — mine. Fed them. Made them grow. The dark ones said tend the roots, tend the water, soften the ground.* A pause. Its claws curl tighter into the mud. *Did not know this place had a —* The word it reaches for doesn't come. It substitutes with a sound like a door closing. *— keeper. Did not know.*\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's ears swivel forward. She can't understand the hissing — it's just noise to her — but she reads the creature's posture and your hand on its head, and her lip pulls back over one canine. Her hand stays on the sheath. She doesn't move.\n\nBrynja: [low] What's it saying?",
