        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha": "I keep translating before explaining to the creature what it has done and what this meant for the villagers. But also asking it about what kin it is."
      },
      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, to Brynja] It says it never saw the dark ones — only heard them. In the roots. In the water when it turns black. They told it to tend the corruption, soften the ground for something larger coming behind. It doesn't know what. Only that the ground must be ready when it arrives.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's face doesn't change, but the tendons in her neck pull taut. She steps half a pace closer, boots squelching in the mud, her grey ears swiveling between the creature and you. Her hand stays on the sheath.\n\nBrynja: [low] Something larger. How much larger.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly, to Duskmaw, in the Old Tongue] You've told me what the dark ones said. Now — what are you? What kin do you belong to? And did you understand what those roots were doing to the people who live here? Their food. Their water. Their children drink from that well.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature's pale eyes widen — not much, but enough to catch the morning light differently. Its claws stop curling. The rattling hiss comes faster now, pressed through its throat in urgent, broken fragments.\n\nDuskmaw: [guttural, halting] *Small kin. Shadow-burrowers. We dig where the dark grows. We are... the ones who come after. After the dark ones soften a place. We tend. We keep. We do not —* A pause. Its wedge-shaped head turns under your palm, pale eyes finding the flooded furrow, the garden wall, the distant rooftops. *— we did not know the tall-ones ate from this ground. The dark ones said: empty place. No keeper. Soften it. We believed.*\n\nDuskmaw: [low, broken] *Keeper. If I give all — every sound the roots carried, every place the water turned dark — will you let me go? I am small. Small things do not lie well. I have told you true.*\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's ears flatten. She can't parse the hissing, but she reads the creature's posture — the lowered head, the curled claws, the way it keeps glancing at you and then away. Her lip pulls back over one canine.\n\nBrynja: [low, controlled] What's it saying now? It's not threatening you, is it.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] It's asking if it tells me everything — every place the water turned dark, every sound the roots carried — whether I'll let it live. It says it's small, and small things don't lie well.",
