      "storyParagraphs": [
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, in the Old Tongue] You gave me everything. So I give you what I promised. Go. You are free.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature's pale eyes widen. Its haunches quiver once — the instinct to bolt warring with the disbelief of a small thing hearing words it has never heard from anything larger. Then its belly lifts from the mud, claws finding purchase, and it begins to rise.",
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly, still in the Old Tongue] But before you do — listen. You said you are a tender. You tend roots, you tend ground, you keep places alive. That is not nothing. What if you did not have to tend corruption to eat? What if the people here — the tall-ones, the ones with the gardens — could share this ground with you and your kin? They work soil. You work soil. You are not so different in what you do.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature freezes mid-rise, one forepaw still in the furrow. Its wedge-shaped head turns back toward you, pale eyes narrowing — not with suspicion, but with the slow, cautious recalibration of something that has only ever known the world as predator and prey.",
        "Duskmaw: [guttural, halting] *Share... ground? With the tall-ones? They do not... they would —* Its throat clicks. *— they would kill us. Small things in their gardens. They have knives. They have dogs.*",
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [gently] They have knives because they are afraid. They have dogs because they are afraid. Fear makes everyone sharp. But I am here, and I am asking you — let me speak to them. Let me ask. If they say yes, you and your kin tend the ground alongside them, not against. No more dark roots. No more blackened water. Just soil, and the things that grow from it.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature's ears lift a fraction from their flat press against its skull. Its pale eyes drift to the garden wall, the green shoots beyond it, the distant rooftops where smoke rises from morning fires. Something moves behind those frost-chip eyes — not hope, not yet, but the shadow of a shape that might become hope if it is not crushed too quickly.",
        "Duskmaw: [low, broken] *The keeper asks. The tall-ones... decide. If they say yes —* A pause so long the furrow water laps against the mud. *— we would tend. We know soil. We know what grows beneath. We could... tell the tall-ones where the ground is sick before it turns black. We could be useful. Not hidden. Useful.*",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> Its head lowers fully into the mud — a slow, deliberate bow, belly flat, claws spread, pale eyes fixed upward on your face.",
        "Duskmaw: [guttural, quiet] *May I go now, keeper? And if I may — I will carry word of you to the dark ones. So they know this ground is claimed. Not empty. Not soft. They will hear your name in the roots, and they will know.*",
        "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You lift your palm from the creature's skull. The morning light catches the fading shimmer of your markings, cherry-blossom branches settling to dormant ink along your nape. You straighten from the crouch, silk robes heavy with furrow water, and turn to Brynja — who has planted herself between the creature and the garden wall, boots grinding in the mud, one hand still on her sheath.",
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly] Brynja. I told it it's free to go. But I also proposed something — and I need your answer before it matters. The creature is a tender. It works soil, reads ground, knows when the earth is sick before it turns. I asked whether its kin and this village could share the land. Help each other. Not as enemies. As neighbors.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's grey ears snap upright. Her amber eyes cut from the creature in the mud to your face, then back to the creature, then to the green shoots on the garden wall — the ones you saved an hour ago. Her jaw works. The tendons in her neck pull taut. Her hand stays on the sheath, but her knuckles have gone white, not from the grip — from the thinking.",
        "Brynja: [low, measured] Neighbors. With the thing that poisoned my well.",
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] The thing that was used to poison your well. There is a difference. It was told this place was empty — no keeper, no people worth naming. It believed that because no one told it otherwise. Now it knows. The question is whether you want it afraid of you, or useful to you.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja's thumb slides along the sheath. Her grey-streaked tail drops low, then slowly lifts. She looks at the flooded furrow where the blackened root-sludge still seeps, then at the garden wall where new green is already pushing through the cleansed soil. Her nostrils flare once. When she speaks, her voice is rough — not angry, but the roughness of someone turning a heavy stone over in her hands and finding the underside cleaner than she expected.",
        "Brynja: [low, firm] If it can tell us when the ground is sick before it turns black — if it can warn us before the roots reach the well again — then I want that. I lost a whole season. Mira lost her bean crop. The Ashfords nearly lost their well. If this thing can stop that from happening again... [she pauses, eyes narrowing at the creature] ...then it earns its place. Not as a guest. As a worker. Same as the rest of us. It gets fed. It gets shelter if it needs it. And it answers to this village — not to the dark ones, not to the roots, not to whatever is coming from the east. Us.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature's pale eyes widen. Its head lifts from the mud, just enough to look past you at Brynja — at the Wolf-Kin woman with the knife and the calloused hands and the voice like a gate closing on its last open inch. It does not understand her words. But it reads her posture, her ears, the set of her shoulders, and the way her hand finally — slowly — slides off the sheath.",
        "Duskmaw: [guttural, to Tsukiko] *The tall-one with the grey ears. She says yes?*",
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, in the Old Tongue] She says yes. But you answer to this village now. Not to the dark ones. Not to the roots. You work the soil with them, you warn them when the ground is sick, and you earn your place. That is the bargain.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> The creature rises fully from the mud. It stands — short, broad, mottled dark fur slicked flat with furrow water — and dips its wedge-shaped head once, a jerky, unpracticed motion that is clearly not a natural gesture but a copied one. Something it has seen other creatures do when a larger thing has shown mercy. Then it turns, not toward the treeline, but toward the north path — and pauses, looking back over its shoulder at you.",
        "Duskmaw: [low, broken] *I will tell the dark ones. The keeper has claimed this ground. The tall-ones have a name for it now. It is not empty. It is not soft.* A pause. *...Thank you, keeper. We have not been... thanked. Before.*",
        "Narrator: <focus:Duskmaw> It moves — not fleeing, not bolting, but padding with a low, deliberate gait along the furrow's edge toward the north path, its broad shoulders hunched, its thick tail dragging a line through the mud. It does not look back again. Within a dozen paces the morning shadows fold around it, and the mottled fur drinks the light until there is nothing left but the faint impression of claws in the wet earth, already filling with water.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja watches the creature go. Her ears stay flat. Her hand hangs at her side now, empty of the sheath, and her fingers open and close once — the reflex of a grip released too recently. The spring breeze carries the smell of turned earth and furrow water across the garden, and a wild rose vine along the wall shivers, new buds catching the light. She turns to you, and her face is not soft, but it is open — the expression of someone who has just agreed to something she does not entirely understand and is not entirely sure she will not regret.",
        "Brynja: [quietly] I hope you're right about that thing. I hope to every god listening I'm right to trust you."
