      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] We may not know each other long or well, but I do know when someone is innocent at heart or foul. And this one? It was simply lied to — and also afraid, because there was something bigger than itself that made promises the creature didn't know were empty. Promises for which most people would agree. Safety. Food. To live. So I do think this village will be fine.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You wring the furrow water from your sleeve with both hands, methodical and unhurried, letting the silk hang heavy and drip. A faint shimmer traces the cherry-blossom branches along your nape as the last of your markings settle to dormant ink. The morning sun catches the wet fabric, and you shake it once, twice, then smooth the folds down with flat palms — the gesture of someone who has cleaned worse things from silk and will again.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja watches you work the water from your robes. Her grey ears twitch once — the involuntary flick of a Wolf-Kin processing something she can't quite name. Her jaw loosens by a degree. She looks at the flooded furrow, the claw-tracks already filling with muddy water, the wild rose buds on the wall. Then back at you — at the shrine maiden wringing ditch water from her sleeves as calmly as if she were folding laundry.\n\nBrynja: [rough, quiet] You talk about it like it's simple. Innocent or foul. As if most things in this world line up that clean.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> She bends and picks up the overturned water bucket, sets it upright, and wipes her muddy hands on her apron. Her tail lifts halfway — not relaxed, but no longer low. She straightens and looks at the north path where the creature disappeared.\n\nBrynja: [low] Safety. Food. To live. [A beat.] I suppose that's what the Ashfords wanted when their well went black. What Mira wanted when her beans rotted. Same promises. Different mouth making them.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> She turns back to you. The wariness hasn't left her eyes, but something else sits alongside it now — not trust, not yet, but the grudging recognition of someone who has watched a stranger do something she would not have done and cannot entirely argue with the result.\n\nBrynja: [quietly] Come on. You're soaked, and Mira will have my hide if I let you catch cold standing in a ditch. There's tea on the common-house hearth, and I still need to show you where the Ashfords' wall sits. If that thing told you the truth about the hollow beneath it, I want to see it with my own eyes before I believe a word.",
