      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, as she falls into step] An innocent heart isn't a clean one, Brynja. It's not about being good — it's about what you do when the choice arrives. The creature in that furrow had no choice. It was told the ground was empty, told to tend it, told it would be fed. It followed the only hand that reached for it. That isn't goodness. That's survival wearing obedience like a skin.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You match her stride along the muddy garden path, silk still heavy with furrow water, your naginata resting across your shoulder. The spring sun warms the back of your neck where the dormant ink of your markings sleeps beneath the collar.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly] A foul heart is different. A foul heart has the choice — and chooses harm anyway. Not because it must. Because it wants to. Because the harm serves it. The dark ones who told that creature to poison your soil — they knew people lived here. They knew the well fed children. They chose it. That is the difference. Not what you are. What you choose when the choice is yours to make.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja walks a half-pace ahead, the water bucket swinging from her fist. Her grey ears rotate back toward your voice without her turning her head. She says nothing for several steps — the quiet of someone chewing on words she isn't ready to spit out or swallow. Her tail sways once, low, then steadies.\n\nBrynja: [rough, after a pause] Choice. [A beat.] The Ashfords didn't choose to have their well turn black. Mira didn't choose to lose her bean crop. Your creature didn't choose to be used. And the ones who gave the orders — they chose. [Her jaw tightens.] Fine. I'll carry that. But if that thing comes back with its kin and any one of them puts a claw toward a child in this village, the choice I make will be a short one.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> She turns left at the garden wall, boots finding the packed-earth lane that skirts the boundary. The wild rose vines along the wall are budding tight, pink-green nubs catching the light. She gestures ahead without slowing — toward a low stone wall half-hidden by overgrown bean trellises, the Ashford plot beyond it.\n\nBrynja: [over her shoulder] The hollow's beneath that wall. If your shadow-burrower told true, the roots run deepest here. I want to see it before we go inside. Then tea — and you're telling Mira and Ivy everything.\n\nNarrator: <arrival> The lane opens onto the village green, and the Wagging Tail sits across the trampled grass like a warm hand held out. Smoke curls from its stone chimney, and the low morning sun catches the whitewashed walls and the faded sign — a painted fox with an exaggerated curl to its tail, grinning over a steaming bowl. The common-room door stands propped open with a river stone, letting the spring air carry out the smell of barley tea and woodsmoke. A few villagers sit on the bench outside, nursing cups. Old Tom Hedley leans in the doorframe, white whiskers catching the light, a rag slung over one shoulder, watching the green with the unhurried patience of a man who has watched this same green for forty years.",
