      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You settle onto the hearth bench, set your naginata against the warm stone, and draw the tea things toward you with both hands. The pot is simple fired clay, thick-walled and heat-stained from years of use. You lift it, test the weight and warmth against your wrist, then pour — a slow, even stream into the first cup, then the second, the barley tea dark and steady. You set the pot down. Adjust the cups so they sit parallel. Place the seed-cake plate between them, centered. Your hands fold in your lap. The gestures are small, unhurried, automatic — the way someone folds a letter they've written a thousand times.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Tom watches from behind the bar, rag over his shoulder, his white whiskers twitching once as you arrange the cups. He doesn't interrupt. When your hands still, he leans forward on the bar with both forearms and speaks low — the voice of a man who keeps his worries behind the cider tap until they're worth naming.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [low, unhurried] The Ashford wall's been giving me a bad feeling all spring. Roots gone dark at the base — not dead, just... wrong. Like they're drinking something they shouldn't be. And I've seen the goblins sniffing round the north track more than once. Three times this month, maybe four. They come, they look, they go. That's not foraging. That's counting.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [plain] So. You came in on the north path, I hear. Brynja doesn't drag strangers through my door before breakfast for nothing. Anything worth worrying about on your way in?",
