      "storyParagraphs": [
        "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly, over the rim of her cup] There was trouble. Four goblins on the north path, laying a hex into the soil — I stopped them, cleansed what they'd poisoned. And something else followed me here. A burrower-creature, sent by what it called the dark ones to soften the ground for something larger coming from the east. It's gone now — we've made other arrangements with it.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Tom doesn't react the way a startled man would. He listens all the way through, whiskers still, then reaches under the bar without hurry. What comes up isn't a blade — it's a leather-bound ledger, cracked at the spine, its pages soft with years of thumbing. He opens it to a dog-eared page, wets the pencil stub behind his ear, and writes a short line beside an older entry. The date, and one word. He sets it back where it lives and picks up his rag again as if nothing happened.",
        "Old Tom Hedley: [dry] Four goblins. A shaman's work in the soil. A burrower under orders. And something bigger coming from the east. [He exhales through his nose.] That explains my spring, right enough.",
        "Old Tom Hedley: [plainly] You'll forgive an old man for keeping count. I did the same work once, a long way from here. Goblins that come to look and go — that's scouting. They don't spend scouts on a poor field. They spend them where something's worth taking.",
        "Narrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> He wipes down a stretch of the bar that doesn't need it, eyes on the green through the open door. Two villagers on the bench catch his glance and drift off toward their plots, cups in hand.",
        "Old Tom Hedley: [low] Brynja will want to see that wall hollow with her own eyes, and she's right to. But hear me, miko — if there's aught buried or bound under Ashford ground, it's been there longer than this spring's trouble. The dark ones didn't start it. They found it."
      ],
