      "storyMessage": "Old Tom Hedley: [quietly, pouring himself a small measure of cider and setting it untouched on the bar] Whatever's buried under that wall — if you find it, don't move it without knowing what it is first. Some things are set where they are for a reason. Even the wrong reasons hold something in place.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You lift your cup and sip, the barley tea warm and faintly sweet against the morning chill still clinging to you. Steam curls between your fingers. You let his words settle before you answer.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [neutrally] That's sound counsel. Things that are buried are often burying something else — or holding a door shut. I've pulled up enough of both to know the difference isn't always visible from above.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Tom nods once, slow, as if you've confirmed something he'd only half-dared to think. He picks up his untouched cider, considers it, and sets it back down in the same spot.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [dry] Thought you'd say something like that. [He taps the bar twice with one knuckle.] Brynja's checking the wall now. When she comes back, you'll want to see what she's found before you go poking anything. Old ground in the Marches has a temper.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You settle back on the hearth bench, cup cradled in both palms, the fire's warmth soaking into your mud-stiff hem. Through the open door, the green lies bright and empty, and beyond the north wall, somewhere past the gardens, Brynja is walking toward whatever the ground has been keeping.",
