      "storyMessage": "Narrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You stand at the edge of the Ashford plot, where the garden soil meets the rough, unhewn stone of the boundary wall. The morning sun catches the fine, silver-etched markings on your naginata, a stark contrast to the jagged, dark aperture Brynja has cleared at the wall's base. The scent of damp earth and something metallic, like old blood and copper, rises from the opening.\n\nBrynja: [whispering] There. Beneath the foundational stones. I had to pull back the briars just to see the edge of it.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You kneel, the silk of your vestments brushing the grass as you peer into the hollow. A slab of basalt, polished to a mirror-sheen by time and pressure, sits wedged deep in the marl. It is covered in a dense, flowing script that seems to move when not looked at directly—not Aerfálan, nor any common tongue of the four powers, but a ritual cypher used to bind tectonic mana.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [calmly] This isn't a grave marker. It’s a seal. A cork in a very old bottle.\n\nBrynja: [nervous] A seal for what? The burrower said the dark ones were coming for the well, but if this was already here...\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You reach out, your fingers hovering just inches from the stone's surface. Even without touching it, you can feel the heat Hedley mentioned—a rhythmic, subterranean pulse that matches the vibration of the ley-spring at the village center. The \"wound\" you sensed in the soil wasn't just goblin corruption; it was the seal itself beginning to hairline-fracture under the weight of the encroaching hexes.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [neutrally] It holds back the pressure of the ley-line beneath us. The goblins weren't just poisoning the crops, Brynja. They were trying to rot the anchor that keeps this spring from becoming a geyser of raw, tainted mana.\n\nBrynja: [gasping] Then we shouldn't have cut those roots! If the fluid was draining—\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [firmly] Cutting the roots saved the well. But it also alerted the stone that its 'food'—the containment of that pressure—is failing.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Brynja> Brynja wipes a bead of sweat from her brow, her ears flattening against her head as she looks back toward the peaceful houses of Whiskerbrook. The village-green is quiet, but the air suddenly feels heavy, charged with the static of a storm that hasn't arrived yet.\n\nBrynja: [low] Tom said not to move it. If we leave it, the corruption finds its way back in. If we fix it... well, I don't know how to fix a stone that talks in heat. Can you?",
