      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] Thank you, Mira. The barley smells wonderful today.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You settle into the chair with a fluid, silent grace that seems out of place among the sturdy, rough-hewn furniture of the inn. Your shrine vestments remain impossibly clean despite your time in the fields, and the faint silver glow of your markings dims to a soft, rhythmic pulse as you find your seat.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Mira Thistledown> Mira carefully fills a second cup, the steam curling around her long, twitching ears as she maintains her attentive, bustling energy. She slides a small plate of golden honey-cakes toward you, her expression a mix of maternal care and the deep-seated awe the village has developed for you.\n\nMira Thistledown: [hopeful] Was the walk back peaceful? After the way the roses bloomed, half the village is convinced the ground itself is singing. Please, miko, tell me if there is anything else you need—a fresh basin of water, more blankets? Anything at all before the sun dips low.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Behind the bar, the heavy thud of a closing ledger echoes through the quiet common-house. Old Tom Hedley leans his thick elbows on the polished wood, his white whiskers twitching as he observes you over the rim of his spectacles with the calm, unhurried gaze of a man who has seen many legends pass through his doors.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [calmly] Don’t let her fuss you into a corner, Lady Tsukiko. She’d have you sleeping on a bed of goose-down and rose petals if she could find enough of ‘em.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> He reaches under the bar for a clean rag and begins to slow-buff a spot on the counter, though his eyes remain fixed on you. There is a weight to his silence, a professional curiosity about whether a being of your power is truly settling in or simply catching her breath before the Marches call her away again.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [slyly] Planning on staying for a proper drink tonight, or are you just humoring Mira’s tea before you go looking for whatever else is buried under our hills?",
