      },
      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] Thank you, Mira. The barley smells wonderful today.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You lift the ceramic cup with both hands, the steam rising to meet the moonstone-gray of your eyes. You take a slow, deliberate sip of the tea, letting the warmth settle before looking up at the rabbit-kin woman with a gaze that is both ancient and genuinely touched.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [gentle] I mostly came to speak my gratitude and appreciation to Mira once again. For her welcoming warmth when I arrived, the meal, but also her hospitality over the last three days.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Mira Thistledown> Mira’s long ears stand straight up, practically vibrating with a sudden, overwhelming joy that brings a bright flush to her cheeks. She clasps her hands together over her apron, her tail twitching in a rapid, rhythmic blur behind her.\n\nMira Thistledown: [cheerful] Oh, Lady Tsukiko! Please, the pleasure has been all mine, truly! To have such a... such a divine guest in our humble village, and to see what you’ve done for the gardens... I’m just happy you found your way to my door.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Old Tom lets out a low, appreciative chuckle, finally tossing the polishing rag onto the back counter and leaning his weight forward. He watches the exchange with a softened expression, the habitual wariness of a frontier innkeeper momentarily eclipsed by the sincerity in the room.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [warmly] Gratitude is a rare currency in the Marches these days, miko. Most folks just take what they can and keep moving before the dust settles. But that sort of talk? That’s the kind that makes a place feel like home.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> He nods toward the teapot, his eyes crinkling at the corners.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [calmly] Is the brew to your liking, then? Mira treats those leaves like they’re made of gold when she knows you’re coming through the door.",
