      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [gentle] The brew is quite perfect, Mira. It has a clarity that reminds me of the mountain springs back home.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Mira Thistledown> Mira beams, her entire face lighting up as she leans in to nudge the plate of honey-cakes a few inches closer to your hand. Her ears dip and sway with a nervous, happy energy, and she watches your expression intently, waiting for you to take another bite.\n\nMira Thistledown: [cheerful] Oh, I’m so glad! And please, Lady Tsukiko, do have more of the cakes! I’ve been refining the recipe these last few days, adding just a touch more clover-honey and a pinch of ground sun-root. I wanted them to be special... something worthy of a miko’s palate.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Old Tom leans back, his heavy wooden chair creaking under his weight as he lets out a long, resonant sigh of contentment. He looks around his quiet, warm common-house, then settles his gaze back on you, his curiosity clearly getting the better of his professional reserve.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [jovial] You’ve a way with words as much as you do with the soil, Lady Tsukiko. It makes a man wonder about that fox region of yours.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> He gestures vaguely with a thick hand toward the window, where the first stars are beginning to prick through the twilight sky.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [calmly] We get all sorts through Whiskerbrook, but never anyone who talks to the earth like it’s an old friend. Do you have any stories from back there? I reckon the folks around here would love to hear about a place where the land is as spirited as you are.\n\nTsukiko Kuzunoha: [softly] Stories... yes. My homeland is a place where the silence is never truly empty, and the trees have long memories. It is quite different from the Marches, yet the heart of the world remains the same.",
