      "storyMessage": "Tsukiko Kuzunoha: [chuckling] The spirits of my home are quite fond of storytelling, though they often prefer riddles to plain speech. They drink the dew of dawn and the moonlight of midsummer, and their laughter sounds like wind-chimes in a storm.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Mira Thistledown> Mira lets out a tiny, enchanted gasp and clasps her hands together, her fingers pressing tight against her chest as if to hold the image of the storm-chimes close. Her long ears quiver with a sudden, intense curiosity, and she leans in until she is almost whispering across the table.\n\nMira Thistledown: [whispering] Wind-chimes in a storm... oh, it sounds so wild and lovely. Can that laughter be heard by anyone who listens closely, Lady Tsukiko? Or is it a secret kept only for those the spirits truly trust?\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> Old Tom chuckles and shakes his head, his eyes crinkling as he stares down into the amber depths of his cider. He swirls the liquid, watching the firelight dance in the glass before looking back up at you with a grin that tugs at his white whiskers.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [amused] Dew and moonlight, eh? Sounds like a diet for poets and ghosts, miko.\n\nNarrator: <focus:Old Tom Hedley> He raises his mug in a mock toast, the wooden handle creaking slightly in his calloused grip.\n\nOld Tom Hedley: [jovial] It makes me wonder, though... would those spirits of yours find my strong apple cider a bit too bold for their tastes? Or do they appreciate a brew with enough kick to remind 'em they're still part of the living world?\n\nNarrator: <focus:Tsukiko Kuzunoha> You watch the steam from your tea curl into the air, the soft light of the tavern hearth reflecting in your moonstone eyes. The village feels tucked away from the rest of the world tonight, the warmth of the common-house creating a quiet sanctuary against the wild hills of the Marches outside.",
