 Tell me, dear, what is needed to expand it to real planetary size with continents, oceans, biomes, and all the rest?\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> The Chamberlain's pen stops. They set it down on the open ledger — not aligned this time, just set down — and look at you. The considering gaze holds for a long beat. Their fingers press flat against the page, steadying themselves against something that is not the pen.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [slowly] Planetary size.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [measured] I want to be clear that I am not questioning your capability. You manifested an entire estate in the time it took me to write one entry. I have stopped drawing conclusions about what you can and cannot do.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> They close the ledger. Their hands rest on its cover, palms flat, as though grounding themselves against the only solid thing in the conversation.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [methodically] What I can tell you is what the Demi-Plane currently is, and what it would need to become. At present, the realm extends roughly to the horizon in every direction from this estate — a clearing, a sky, and the ground beneath both. That is the entirety of it. No curvature. No depth beyond the topsoil. No geological strata, no ocean basins, no atmospheric layers. The sky is a painted ceiling that looks infinite and is not.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [evenly] To expand to true planetary scale — continents, oceans, biomes, a functioning day-night cycle, weather systems, tectonic stability — you would need to address, at minimum, the following.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> They reopen the ledger to a fresh page and begin writing as they speak — not in columns this time, but in a numbered list, each entry deliberate.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [counting] First, spatial expansion. The Demi-Plane's borders are defined by your connection to it. Pushing them outward to planetary circumference requires sustained, repeated investment — not a single act, but a series of them, each extending the boundary further. The estate was one act because it already existed in your memory. A world does not.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [counting] Second, geological foundation. Continents require depth — rock strata, mineral veins, magma chambers, a mantle. Without it, the ground is a shell. Anything heavy enough — a mountain, a city — would eventually punch through into nothing. You would need to build downward as well as outward.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [counting] Third, hydrosphere. Oceans, rivers, lakes, groundwater, and the water cycle that connects them. The stream behind your estate appeared because you remembered it. An ocean cannot be remembered into being — it must be created, filled, and given currents, tides, and salinity gradients.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [counting] Fourth, atmosphere and celestial mechanics. A day-night cycle requires a light source that moves, or the illusion of one. Weather requires atmospheric pressure differentials, temperature gradients, and moisture. The current sky is decorative. It would need to become functional.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [counting] Fifth, biosphere. Biomes — forest, grassland, desert, tundra, wetland — each requires the soil, climate, and microbial foundation to sustain the life you would place in it. Otherwise you are arranging scenery, not building a living world.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [steadily] These are not obstacles. They are sequence. Each one builds on the one before it. Spatial expansion makes room. Geology gives it bones. Water gives it blood. Atmosphere gives it breath. Biosphere gives it skin.\n\nNarrator: <focus:The Chamberlain> They set the pen down again and look up at you across the tea tray. The dry wit is still there, but buried under something more careful — the look of someone who has stopped planning and started listening.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [directly] The first concrete step is surveying what we have — the estate grounds and the clearing beyond — to map the best sites for the first terrain expansion. Where the ground is already most responsive to you, where the borders are thinnest, where the soil has the most depth to work with.\n\nThe Chamberlain: [calmly] So. Do you want me to begin that survey now? Or do you intend to shape the land yourself, the way you did the estate?\n\n**World Setting**: Aerfála is an anime-fantasy world governed by light-novel logic. Adventurers' Guild branches stand in every major town, posting quests ranked F through S. Magic is colorful, abundant, and color-coded ...\n"
